"""Original Security teaching packs. Each body is instructor prose."""

BODIES = {
    'Vendor penetration testing': {
        'body': "A permitted test against a vendor is a bounded attempt to break in and show impact, and it happens only after someone with authority has agreed in writing. The contract may demand that test, or your team may commission it while you examine a supplier you already pay. The product you are allowed to keep is a current summary: the firm, the dates, the severity of each finding, and whether the high findings were tried again after repair. Raw records that belong to the vendor's other customers stay out of the package. A contract clause that only lets you inspect controls is a different power. A review that reads evidence and never exploits a flaw is a different product. The work earns this name when someone actually tries a path.\n\nThe attempt starts from a written boundary rather than from a salesperson's enthusiasm. That page names the hosts, the hours, the zones nobody may touch, and the person to call if a service wobbles. Life-safety equipment stays outside the boundary even when a path toward it looks easy. Testers who receive diagrams and a privileged account are answering how far a stolen administrator laptop could go. Testers who receive only a public name are answering what a stranger can learn. When some facts are shared and others are withheld, the summary has to say which facts were provided. A technical probe, a visit to the building, or both can sit in one engagement, and the summary should name the kind that was actually run.\n\nOn a clinic mail contract, the March summary listed an admin page on the hosting portal that asked for no second factor, marked high, seen on 12 March. The retest line dated 2 April said the page then rejected a password alone. The package held no mailboxes from the vendor's other hospital tenants. Page one of the rules forbade the imaging network and printed the vendor operations mobile. Defenders treated the summary as a question list. They asked who owned the repair, whether a sister portal had the same page, and what date the screenshots would be destroyed.\n\nA defender files the firm, the window, the severity scale, and the retest result beside every high finding. A summary from several years ago does not describe the system you are about to trust with clinic records. An open high finding past its repair window belongs on the risk register with an owner and a review date. This work does not replace the investigation you do before you sign, and it does not replace careful daily operation after the system is live. Students often swap it with a right to inspect or with an outside opinion that never tried to break in. The separating fact is simple: someone attempted to show impact inside a written scope, and you can point at the current summary. A conference-room discussion with no live probe is a tabletop, and that discussion does not show impact either.",
        'r0': "The engagement defines targets, methods, hours, a data-handling ban, and a stop condition so the work cannot drift into life-safety gear or into another customer's records. Artifacts name the testing firm, the calendar window, the severity scale, the condition that was actually shown, and the retest status of each high finding. A known-environment run supplies diagrams and credentials and speaks to depth, such as what a stolen administrator laptop could reach. An unknown-environment run withholds those aids and speaks to outsider discovery. One clinic summary cited an admin page on the public portal over tcp/443, dated 12 March, with a passing retest on 2 April. The exercise remains an attempt to show impact with permission, so it does not issue an opinion on control design across a sampled period. Screenshot storage and a destruction date belong in the evidence rules.",
        'r1': "A vendor test needs written permission first. The scope names hosts, hours, and a stop. Testers try to show real impact. You keep a current findings summary. Other customers' raw files stay out. An inspection clause only lets you look. An outside review may never break in. The contract may require the test. You may also test a supplier you pay. The summary names the firm and the dates. High findings need a retest line. Life-safety gear stays off limits. A talk with no live step is different. Old summaries do not describe today.",
        'r2': "A theater hires a visitor to learn which backstage doors open before curtain, and the visitor must stop at a rope. The visitor tries the marked doors and writes down only the ones that opened. The note never includes another show's audience list. If the visitor only sits in the green room and talks through a break-in, that seated talk is a different rehearsal. A season contract that lets a producer walk the halls and look is a different paper, because looking is not forcing a lock. In the real engagement, the visitor is the tester, the rope is the written scope, and the short note is the findings summary. The audience list that stays backstage is the other customers' raw data, which that summary is not allowed to carry.",
        'r4': "Get written permission before any probe of the vendor. Name the hosts, the hours, and the forbidden zones. Keep life-safety equipment outside the test. Ask for a summary dated inside the contract window. Reject any package that includes other customers' raw records. Record the retest result beside each high finding. Compare the summary with claims the vendor made on a form. Store screenshots under the destruction date you agreed. Tell the on-call defender before a live step begins.",
        'exam': "Choose this label when a stem shows a permitted attempt to show impact and a current summary the customer may see. Do not choose it for a clause that only allows an inspection, or for an outside review that never exploits a flaw. A conference-room discussion with no live step is a tabletop, not this test. The summary is not a dump of other customers' raw records. A known-environment result and an unknown-environment result answer different questions, and the stem should say which facts the tester was given.",
    },
    'Right to audit': {
        'body': "A right to audit is a contract term that lets you check a vendor's controls, or lets you accept a current independent report in place of your own visit. The clause is the power. The report is one way the power can be satisfied, and the report is not the clause. Terms usually name the notice you must give, the hours you may come, how often you may come, and a duty to keep other customers' data out of your hands. A fresh outside packet is often allowed as a substitute so dozens of customers are not queued at one loading dock. The power still fails its purpose when nobody reads the exceptions, the covered period, and the issue date.\n\nBefore a renewal, the contract owner chooses a visit or an accepted packet and writes that choice where the next owner can find it. Attention belongs on controls that match the service you buy: who has access, whether logs exist, how secrets are protected, how fast incidents are told, and how subcontractors are watched. A demand for an unrelated product line, or for raw tenant files from other clients, turns a fair clause into a fight. If the text never says what substitute report will do, a later argument appears about whether a logo is enough. The file you retain holds the clause, the notice you sent, the packet you accepted, and the gaps you still asked the vendor to explain.\n\nDuring selection for a clinic host, section 8.2 allowed one visit a year on fifteen business days' notice, or a current outside report whose review period had ended within the past twelve months. The renewal folder held that report. The exceptions page said logging was off for a backup administrator role. The cover logo was clean. The security lead wrote the logging gap onto the renewal list and asked the vendor for a dated repair plan. Nobody treated the logo as the answer. A quarterly check of who still holds admin rights kept its own name, because one yearly look does not watch the tenant between visits.\n\nUse the clause while you can still walk away, which is before the data moves, and keep using it on the calendar after you sign. Visitors need a confidentiality promise of their own so the inspection does not become a new leak. Gaps become contract remedies only when the agreement ties them to repair time, credits, or a right to leave. Do not hide a break-in test inside this clause. If you want a permitted attempt to show impact, put targets and hours in the rules and on the job page. Students swap this power with the break-in test and with the report's logo. The separating fact is permission to look, or a defined substitute, plus whether anyone read the gaps.",
        'r0': "Implement the clause with a notice rule, a frequency cap, a confidentiality limit, and either an on-site examination or acceptance of a defined outside packet. Acceptance criteria should name the report type, the maximum age of the period end, and whether a bridge letter is required after that date. One clinic clause allowed a single visit each year on fifteen business days' notice, or a report whose period ended within twelve months. Sampling stays on controls mapped to the purchased service: access administration, logging, encryption, incident notice, and subcontractor oversight. Evidence you keep includes the clause, the notice, the packet or the visit notes, and an exception log with owners. The clause does not authorize a break-in, extraction of other tenants' files, or a standing presence in the vendor network.",
        'r1': "A right to audit is a contract clause. It lets you inspect the vendor's controls. A current outside report may stand in. The clause is not the report itself. The clause is not a break-in test. Notice and how often you may come are written. Other customers' files stay out of your hands. You still have to read the exceptions. A clean logo is not the evidence. Put the choice in writing before renewal. One yearly look does not watch every day. Tie a gap to the remedy the contract gives. Keep a date for the next look.",
        'r2': "An orchard buyer may walk the rows before the next season, or may accept a crop survey written by someone who is not the grower's salesperson. The permission sits on the purchase paper. The survey booklet is not the permission, and a dare to shake every tree at midnight is not the permission either. A buyer who never opens the blemish list has only admired the wax seal. Walks happen in daylight, with warning, and they skip the neighbor's grove. One walk a harvest does not notice a broken gate in midsummer. In the contract, the walk is your visit and the survey is the independent report you may accept instead. The blemish list is the exceptions page, which still has to be read before you renew.",
        'r4': "Write the visit-or-report choice before any records move. Accept a substitute report only inside the freshness window the clause names. Read the exceptions page before you renew. Limit the visit to the service you actually buy. Forbid the team from taking other customers' files. Put the next review on a calendar. Keep this clause separate from a break-in scope. Require a confidentiality promise from every visitor. Tie each gap to the remedy the contract actually gives.",
        'exam': 'Use this label when the stem shows a clause that lets you inspect controls or accept a current outside report in its place. Do not use it for a scoped attempt to break in. Do not use it for a logo when the exceptions page was ignored. The clause is the power, and the report is only one way to satisfy the power. A quarterly admin review between visits is a different control. Reading the period, the issue date, and the gaps is part of the answer.',
    },
    'Independent assessments': {
        'body': 'Independent assessments are done by someone outside the team being checked. The reviewer is not the sales staff chasing a signature, and not the people who built the control. The review asks whether controls were designed for a stated rule and, when the packet covers a stretch of time, whether those controls actually operated. A design look on one date and an operating look across a period answer different questions, so the cover has to name which product you hold. Trust sits in the scope, the dates, the tests, and the exceptions. A logo, a letterhead, or a slide that only names a standard is not the review.\n\nThe packet describes the system, the controls tested, the sample, and the duties the customer still owns. Those duties often include adding and removing your own users. If that list is ignored, a clean opinion never covers your side of the work. A report whose period ended many months ago shows an older system unless management adds a short note about changes since the end date. The note does not replace the next full review. Scope text decides whether your tenant was even in the sample. A clean letter about another product, or another region, does not describe the service you might buy. If a contract lets you accept the packet instead of a visit, the type and the age still have to match the clause.\n\nOn 4 April, Jordan opened the hosting firm\'s current report for the clinic patient portal. The cover opinion looked clean. Page 12 said logging was off for the backup admin role through the period that ended on 30 November. The scope paragraph named the portal and skipped the billing add-on a salesperson had pitched by phone. Jordan wrote ticket 4417 with the words "backup admin logging off, period end 30 November, portal only." Tests in the same packet said stored files used AES-256. Nothing on page 12 was a permitted attempt to break in. Nothing on the cover was the contract clause that allows a visit.\n\nBefore renewal, read each exception and write it down with an owner. Compare a claim of encryption on every path with any exception that still shows a cleartext transfer. List the controls the packet hands back to you, and check that your own procedure performs them. Ask for the full description and the tests, not a one-page badge. When the period is stale, ask what changed, and do not treat that reply as a fresh review. Leave other customers\' raw records out of the folder. Accept the exception, require a repair, or walk away, and file that choice where the renewal owner can find it. Students mix this packet with a break-in test and with the contract power to look. The separating fact is the reviewer: someone outside the team being checked, reading exceptions, scope, and period rather than the logo.',
        'r0': "A current packet should name the reviewer, the system, the sample, and whether the opinion is design only or covers operation across a period. For the clinic portal, page 12 recorded logging off for the backup admin role through a period that ended on 30 November, while storage tests cited AES-256. Customer duties in the same packet still included removing the clinic's own users. A short note after 30 November can explain changes, and that note does not restart the period. Scope that names only the portal does not cover a billing add-on. The packet can stand in for a visit only when the clause names that report type and a freshness limit, such as a period end within twelve months. It does not record a permitted break-in, and a cover logo is not a control result.",
        'r1': 'An outside person reviews the controls. That person is not on the sales staff. Read the exceptions, not the cover logo. A one-day design look is not a period look. Old reports need a note about later changes. That note is not a new review. Check duties the report hands back to you. A clean letter can describe the wrong product. This packet is not a break-in test. A visit clause is a different power. You still have to read the gaps. Write each gap with an owner. A logo alone is not evidence.',
        'r2': 'A diner trusts the walk-in cooler more than the poster on the door. The inspector is not the cook, and the inspector is not the person selling party contracts. The inspector opens the cooler, writes the warm shelf on a form, and ignores the cheerful painting outside. A form from last spring does not describe a cooler replaced in the fall. A form about the bakery truck does not describe the dining room. A burglar trying the alley lock is a different visit, and a lease line that lets the diner walk the kitchen is a different paper. In this review, the inspector is the outside reviewer and the warm shelf is the exception you still have to read. The poster is only the logo, which never stands in for that exception.',
        'r4': "Read the scope, the period end, and every exception before renewal. Write each exception with an owner. Map duties in the packet onto your own procedures. Reject a packet that is only a logo. Ask what changed if the period is stale, and do not file that note as a new review. Do not edit the vendor report. Do not collect other customers' raw records. Compare encryption claims with any cleartext exception. Keep this packet separate from a break-in summary and from the visit clause. File the selection decision where renewal staff can find it.",
        'exam': 'Choose this when someone outside the team being checked reviewed the controls and the useful detail is an exception, a scope, or a period. Do not choose it because a cover logo looks clean. Do not choose it for a permitted attempt to break in and show impact. Do not choose it for a contract clause that merely lets you visit or accept a report in place of a visit. A design opinion and a period opinion answer different questions. A note about changes after the period ended is not a new review.',
    },
    'Supply-chain analysis': {
        'body': "Supply-chain analysis asks who sits upstream of the company you pay, and what happens if that upstream party stalls, fails, or is compromised. It is not the score sheet that picks the direct supplier. It is not the quarterly watch that starts after the signature. A single source is concentration, not a trivia line under the price. A delay and a compromise are different stories: a poisoned update can arrive on schedule and still be hostile. The work lists both availability threats and integrity threats against each critical upstream name.\n\nStart from the service you depend on and walk one or two hops outward: hosting, identity, a chip, a library, freight, and the firm that remotes in to patch. For each hop, record whether a substitute exists, how long a swap would take, and which contract forces notice when the hop changes. Stopping at the logo on the invoice never leaves the company you already know. A questionnaire can start the list, and then you challenge contradictions, such as a claim of many suppliers beside a drawing that shows one. The map belongs before records move, because discovering a single source after go-live is an expensive surprise. The map continues when a subcontractor is added, a region shifts, or a component you counted on is swapped.\n\nA clinic badge program depended on one vendor, and that vendor's diagram named a single chip foundry. The team asked what a six-month stall would do to new badges, to hiring, and to door access. They also asked what a tainted chip batch would do if the badges arrived on time and still failed. The note named a second foundry that could be qualified in ten weeks, and it named the contract line that requires notice before a foundry change. No raw files from the badge vendor's other clients were collected. The decision on the risk record was to qualify the second source before the next hiring wave, with a review date in six months.\n\nEvidence is a map with names, not a slogan about how seriously anyone takes risk. Software hops include libraries, the build system, and who may publish an update your hosts will trust. A payment hop asks whether a checkout script from another firm can see a full card number before a token replaces it. A clinical hop asks whether a transcription partner can export names into a place your inventory never listed. Finish with a decision: accept the concentration, require a second source, change the design so an upstream failure hurts less, or decline the supplier. Students swap this map with the direct-vendor score and with the watch after signature. The separating fact is the upstream name, the stall or compromise story, and a decision that looks past the company you pay.",
        'r0': "Build a named map of upstream parties, substitutes, swap time, data touched, and both stall scenarios and compromise scenarios. Treat a single-source component as concentration. In one badge program, a single chip foundry meant a six-month stall could stop new badges and freeze door access together. A tainted batch is a separate case because the parts can arrive on time and still be hostile. Software maps include libraries, build pipelines, and the publishers allowed to ship updates the estate will trust. Payment maps ask whether a third-party script sees the full card number before tokenization. Evidence is the map plus a decision to accept, diversify, redesign, or decline, and a review date. Do not collect other customers' raw records while you draw the map.",
        'r1': "Look upstream of the company you pay. Ask who supplies that company. A stall and a compromise are different worries. One chip source can stop badges and doors. Write down a substitute and the swap time. A drawing that shows one source beats a vague claim. Do this before records move. Keep looking when a subcontractor changes. Include libraries and update paths. Name what each hop can see. Record the decision and the next review. Skip other customers' raw files. This is not the direct score sheet. This is not the quarterly watch.",
        'r2': "A bicycle shop asks where the brake pads come from, then asks who grows the only rubber the pad maker can buy. A flood at that one estate stops every safe bike, even though the shop never signed the estate's paper. A tainted batch is a different worry than a late truck, because the pads arrive on time and still fail in the rain. The shop wants a second grove, a longer lead time, or a different brake, and it writes the choice on the wall. Checking only the brand painted on the frame never leaves the bike you already paid for. In the real map, the estate is the upstream party and the flood is the stall or the compromise. The choice on the wall is the decision to accept the concentration, add a second source, or walk away.",
        'r4': "Draw the map one hop past the company on the invoice. Name each single source and the stall story. Add the compromise path, not only the outage. Ask which contract forces notice of a subcontractor change. Keep other customers' raw files out of the folder. Write the decision as accept, diversify, redesign, or decline. Put the next review on a calendar. Name an owner beside each concentrated hop. Check verbal claims against the real diagram. Keep this map separate from the direct-vendor score sheet.",
        'exam': 'Use this label when the stem looks past the company you pay to an upstream stall or compromise. A six-month chip delay that stops badges is this analysis. Do not use it for a price-and-control comparison among hosts you might hire. Do not use it for the quarterly admin check after you sign. The answer wants the upstream name, the consequence, and a decision. A poisoned update that arrives on time is still an upstream integrity problem.',
    },
    'Vendor selection': {
        'body': 'Vendor selection is choosing a supplier before the contract. Price shares the score sheet with protection, logging, how fast bad news is told, where the data will live, which subcontractors will touch it, and how you can leave. A cheaper host can lose when it cannot meet that floor, even after a charming demo. The choice finishes before any live record moves. It is not a map of who sits upstream of the company you might pay. It is not the watch that begins after the ink is dry.\n\nName the data you would hand over, the availability you need, and the notice times you cannot soften. Ask each candidate for a current outside report, a subcontractor list, and a sample of how a serious incident is timed. Treat a questionnaire as a lead. If the form says encryption on every path and an exhibit still shows a cleartext nightly copy, follow that clash before anyone signs. Prefer a design that stores a token rather than a full card number, and reject a design that keeps the security code after a payment is approved. Exit terms belong on the same page: return of the files, deletion, help during a move, and how long copies may remain.\n\nOn 19 May the clinic compared two payroll hosts before any contract draft went out. The west host was cheaper by 18,000 dollars a year. Its packet could not show AES-256 on stored payroll files, and its sample log kept no admin sign-in line. The north host met both points, and its exit clause returned files within 30 days of notice. Priya, the data owner, filed selection note 19 with the line "west rejected, no AES-256 at rest, no admin log." Purchasing did not make that call alone. The payroll records were still on the clinic side of the line.\n\nFreeze the score before the first record moves. A verbal promise that never lands in the agreement does not count, including a casual line about response time with no clock and no remedy. A secrecy promise only limits gossip. It does not replace the control requirements. A memo about a joint drill is not the contract for ongoing data duties. Write why the winner won in words a later reader can follow without the slide deck. If the winner changes a subcontractor or a region before the start date, open the comparison again. Keep raw files that belong to other clients out of the diligence folder. Students call every vendor conversation this choice. Looking upstream for a single chip or library is a different map, and watching admin lists after the signature is the later watch. The separating fact is the moment: a supplier is chosen before the contract, security can beat a lower price, and the records have not moved.',
        'r0': 'Score candidates before any contract is signed and before records move. Protection, logging, notice clocks, subcontractor lists, data location, exit terms, and price share one sheet, and price does not outrank a missing floor. In the 19 May comparison the cheaper host, lower by 18,000 dollars a year, could not show AES-256 on stored payroll files and kept no admin sign-in line. The winner returned files within 30 days of notice. Contradictions on a form are chased into exhibits before signature. A secrecy slip does not replace those control terms. Evidence is the score, the rejection line, and open gaps with owners. Upstream concentration and the quarterly watch after signature answer different questions and stay off this sheet.',
        'r1': "Choose the supplier before you sign. Weigh protection, not price alone. A cheaper host can lose. Score logging, notice time, and exit terms. Read outside reports rather than logos. Chase a form answer that fights an exhibit. Prefer a token over a full card number. Do not keep the security code after payment. A secrecy slip is not the control. This choice is not the upstream map. Watching after signing is a later job. File reasons a later reader can follow. Keep other clients' raw files out. Leave the records unmoved until you sign.",
        'r2': 'A warehouse manager walks two trucking yards before any hauling contract is signed. The cheaper yard has a lower chalk price and a gate that will not latch. The manager watches how visitors are logged and how fast a truck can be sent back if the goods are wrong. A friendly lunch does not replace that walk. A map of who mines the fuel is a different question, and a monthly visit after the trucks already run is a later habit. The goods stay in the home warehouse during the walk. In the real choice, the yards are the candidate suppliers and the gate that will not latch is a control the cheaper one cannot meet. The walk happens before the contract, while your records are still on your side.',
        'r4': "Name the data before you compare hosts. Score protection and exit terms beside price. Reject a cheaper host that misses the floor, including encryption you require. Chase form contradictions into the exhibits before anyone signs. Require a token for card numbers if card data will move. Write the winning reasons plainly. Do not sign yet. Do not move any live record yet. Keep a secrecy slip from standing in for the control terms. Open the score again if a subcontractor changes before the start date. Leave other clients' raw files out of the folder.",
        'exam': 'Choose this when a supplier is selected before the contract and protection can beat a lower price. The records have not moved yet. Do not choose it for a map of an upstream party who might stall or be compromised. Do not choose it for incident reviews, admin lists, or report age after the signature. A secrecy promise is not the control score. A form answer is not proof during this choice. A memo of shared intent is not the contract. The cheaper option loses when AES-256 on stored files is missing.',
    },
    'Vendor monitoring': {
        'body': "Vendor monitoring is what you do after the contract starts. The work tracks incident notices, the age of outside reports, who still holds admin rights, and whether the live service still matches what you signed. The original score sheet picked the supplier. This loop asks whether that choice is still true on a dated list. A meeting with no names, no date, and no owner is not the loop.\n\nPull the artifacts the contract already lets you see. Compare a current outside report, incident notices inside the agreed clock, and proof that last quarter's exceptions moved with the service you actually bought. On a clinic tenant the concrete check is the admin list: which supplier staff still hold admin rights, and which of those people should already be gone. A missed response on a serious incident is written down as the credit the contract allows, not as a story someone remembers. A new subcontractor that the original score never weighed is a change, not a footnote. Your own side of a shared duty counts too. If the report told you to remove your users and you did not, the miss is yours.\n\nOn 12 June, Renee pulled the admin list for the clinic tenant on the payroll host. Fourteen names still had admin rights. Two of them should have been gone: a contractor who left on 3 May, and a night operator taken off the staff list on 28 May. She wrote review note 12 with both names, the leave dates, and a request to remove the rights that day. The same folder held the host report, whose test period had ended on 31 January, so she asked for a short note on changes since that date. She did not treat the note as a new review. An engineer offered a sample mailbox from another client, and she refused it.\n\nPut the loop on a calendar with a named person for the admin list, the report, and the credit log. A host of clinical records gets a tighter cadence than a vendor that never sees sensitive files. Still check a low-risk vendor on a slow cadence, because a tool that once held only brochures can later hold a file share. When a high finding sits past its repair window, escalate it. When the service no longer matches the contract, open the exit terms instead of quietly absorbing the change. Do not rewrite the agreement during the review itself. Students treat the sales meeting as if it were this ongoing check. Choosing the supplier before the contract is the earlier decision. Naming an upstream party who could stall or be compromised is a different map. The separating fact is the date: the contract has started, and the evidence is a dated admin list, a fresh or stale report, a missed clock, or a subcontractor that was not on the original score.",
        'r0': "After the contract starts, review on a set cadence: incident notices, report age, admin rights, missed clocks, and scope drift. On 12 June the clinic tenant admin list, exported from the host portal over tcp/443, showed 14 admin names. Two were stale: a contractor gone since 3 May and an operator removed on 28 May. The outside report period had ended on 31 January, so a change note was requested and was not treated as a new review. A missed one-hour response is a credit entry, not a memory. A subcontractor absent from the original score is logged the week it appears. Other clients' mailboxes are refused. This loop is not the score finished before signature and not the upstream map.",
        'r1': 'Start this work after the contract begins. Track incidents and the age of reports. See who still holds admin rights. Remove names that should already be gone. Write down a missed response clock. Note a subcontractor the score never had. This is not the choice made before signing. This is not the upstream map. A meeting with no list is not enough. Put names and dates on a calendar. Include duties the report gave back to you. Open exit terms when the service drifts. Refuse raw files from other clients. A high finding past its repair date needs an owner.',
        'r2': "A clinic linen contract is already signed, and someone still walks the laundry each month. The walk looks at which drivers still hold a key to the dock and collects keys from drivers who quit. A torn sheet that was supposed to get a call within the hour is written in a notebook if the call comes late. A new wash plant, missing from the paper you signed, gets named on the walk. The walker does not read another clinic's labels while standing in the steam. The walk is not the tour that picked the laundry. In the real work, the monthly walk is the review after the contract starts and the dock key is an admin right that should have been removed. The late call in the notebook is a missed clock you still write down.",
        'r4': "Set a named day for the review after the contract starts. Export the admin list and remove rights that should already be gone. Log credits when a response clock is missed. Compare the outside report period with the freshness you require. Write down subcontractors that were not in the original score. Do not rewrite the contract during the review. Do not absorb a drift quietly. Escalate a high finding past its repair window. Include duties the packet assigned to you. Refuse other clients' raw mail. Keep these notes separate from the original selection sheet.",
        'exam': "Choose this when the contract has already started and the evidence is a dated admin list, a report age, a missed clock, or a new subcontractor. Do not choose it for the comparison that picked the supplier before signature. Do not choose it for an upstream stall or compromise. A friendly meeting with no list and no date is not the review. Duties the report assigned to you still count. Refusing another client's raw mail is part of doing this carefully. Drift that breaks the contract opens exit terms rather than fading into habit.",
    },
    'Questionnaires': {
        'body': 'Questionnaires collect vendor answers about controls, and those answers are not proof. The useful moment is a contradiction: the form says one thing, and a contract exhibit, a diagram, or the live system says another. A form that is filed and never opened is only paper. The header logo does not decide the label. The questions do, and so does the follow-up. A shared industry form and a short list you wrote yourself can both carry this name.\n\nEach question should match the service you might buy. Access removal, encryption, logging, incident notice, subcontractors, data location, and retention are the usual lines. A yes is stronger when it points at something you may later request, such as a policy, a sample log, a current outside report, or a diagram. Card questions ask whether the full card number is stored, whether screens shorten it, and whether the security code remains after payment is approved. Answers written by sales staff who never see the system deserve a second pass by someone who does. A blank, a late reply, or an evasive line is a gap with an owner, not a polite omission.\n\nOn 8 August the clinic sent a short form to a backup vendor. Question 14 said AES-256 on every transfer. Exhibit B of the draft contract still described a nightly file copy in cleartext over ftp, port 21, from the clinic file server to the vendor drop folder. Samir wrote follow-up 14 the same day and recorded that the form claimed encryption everywhere while exhibit B was cleartext on port 21, so the answer was not proof. The vendor later offered a current outside report instead of a long essay. The team accepted that swap only because the report scope included the file transfer, and the exceptions page was still read.\n\nScore the form as one column on the selection sheet, and send a shorter form again after the contract starts if the service changes. Do not demand raw records that belong to other customers as proof. Record the responder name and role so a later reader knows the page was not an anonymous brochure. Close the file only when each contradiction is resolved, accepted, or used to drop the candidate. A form cannot set a measured response time with a credit when the clock is missed. That promise belongs on a service schedule. A form also cannot say what a test may do, what is forbidden, and whom to call if something breaks. Those limits belong on the engagement page. Students treat a completed form as evidence that the controls exist. The separating fact is that these pages collect answers, and the answers are not proof until something else agrees with them.',
        'r0': 'Treat the form as a claim log. Question 14 on the 8 August backup form said AES-256 on every transfer. Exhibit B still required a nightly cleartext copy over ftp on port 21. That pair is a contradiction, and the form is not proof while it stands. Each yes should point at a policy, a sample log, a diagram, or a current outside packet. Card questions cover storage of the full number, shortening on screens, and deletion of the security code after authorization. The responder name and role are recorded. Blanks are gaps. A packet may replace a custom essay only when its scope includes the asked transfer. The form does not create a measured credit for a missed clock, and it does not list allowed and forbidden test actions.',
        'r1': "These forms collect vendor answers. Answers are not proof. A contradiction is the useful part. Match questions to the service you might buy. Point each yes at a document you can request. Ask where card numbers are stored. Ask if security codes remain after payment. Get the responder name and role. A blank line is a gap. Sales staff may need a technical second pass. Do not demand other clients' raw files. A form is not a response credit. A form is not the page of test limits. Close the file only after each conflict has a result.",
        'r2': 'A kitchen asks a caterer to fill a card before a banquet. The card says the soup is kept cold and the meat is dated. That card is a stack of answers, and the answers are not the cooler. The cook opens the cooler because one line claims every tray is iced while the delivery note still describes a warm bin. A card filled in the car park deserves a second look from the cook. The card is not the clock on the wall that says when dinner must land, and it is not the sheet of tasks the crew may attempt. In the real check, the card is the form of vendor answers and the warm bin is the contradiction you still have to chase. The cooler, not the card, is what turns an answer into proof.',
        'r4': "Send questions scoped to the service you might buy. Record the responder name and role. Treat every yes as a lead until a document agrees. Chase contradictions into exhibits and diagrams. Ask about card storage and security codes. Log each blank as a gap with an owner. Do not rewrite the vendor answers. Do not sign on the form alone. Refuse requests for other customers' raw files. Accept an outside packet only when it covers the question you asked. Close the form only when each conflict has a written result.",
        'exam': 'Choose this when the artifact is a structured set of vendor answers about controls. The answers are not proof. A contradiction with an exhibit or a diagram is the clue to follow. Do not choose this for a clause that lets you visit. Do not choose this for a permitted break-in scope. Do not choose this for a measured response time and a credit when the clock is missed. A logo on the form does not change the label. A blank, a late reply, or an evasive line is still a gap.',
    },
    'Rules of engagement': {
        'body': "Rules of engagement say what a test or a vendor may do, what is forbidden, and who to call if something breaks. They are the operational boundary for one activity. They are not the questionnaire that only asks about controls. They are not the measurable uptime promise with a credit when a clock is missed. Without the boundary page, a helpful test becomes an incident of your own making. The page covers timing, targets, data handling, a stop condition, and a letter a person can show if a guard or a colleague challenges them. The same idea applies to supplier staff on your network when the contract bothers to say so.\n\nSuccess is stated in plain terms: show reach, stop before destruction, and leave a summary rather than a pile of other people's files. Primary account numbers, security codes, and clinical exports stay in the environment even if a path makes them visible. Hours are set so a scan does not collide with a backup or a morning clinic rush unless that collision is the point and everyone agreed. The page names the authorizer, the tester lead, and the defender lead so a late page has a human on each side. A halt word stops the work immediately when patient care, safety, or an unrelated outage is at risk. The page also says what evidence may be kept, where screenshots live, and when they are destroyed.\n\nA clinic exercise allowed a scan of the public website and forbade any touch of the life-safety network. The on-call number sat on the first page, next to the halt word. The version number was 4, and the later summary cited version 4 so a reader could see which limits bound the work. When a observer wanted to add a subnet on Wednesday, the named authorizer had to approve it. A chat message was not enough. The defender on call could open the same page the tester carried. Posting the target list on a public board would have handed out the map, so the page stayed with the people who needed it.\n\nA live exercise and a tabletop are different. In a tabletop, people talk through decisions and nobody sends a payload or walks a lobby. A physical attempt adds safety: no force that could injure a person, no blocking of an emergency exit, and a stop line such as the inner lobby. An awareness exercise that sends a harmless practice message says the point is reporting and that nobody is shamed. These rules do not replace a master contract's liability terms or a job page's dates. If they conflict with the job page, reconcile them in writing before the first packet. Students confuse this boundary with a control questionnaire and with an uptime credit. The separating fact is the allowed action, the forbidden zone, and the person to call.",
        'r0': "Specify permitted methods, explicit prohibitions, time windows, targets, data-handling bans, emergency contacts, a halt condition, evidence retention, and the authorizers for both sides. A clinic exercise allowed a scan of tcp/443 on the public site and excluded the life-safety network, with the on-call number on page one. Card numbers, security codes, and clinical content could not be taken out. Physical work adds injury constraints, emergency-exit integrity, and a geographic stop line. Live activity is separate from a discussion-only tabletop, and the summary cites the version that bound the work, such as version 4. A mid-test scope add requires the named authorizer rather than an informal chat. These rules do not replace standing liability terms or the job's dates.",
        'r1': 'Write what the test may do. Write what the test must not do. Put the emergency contact on page one. A public site can be in bounds. Life-safety gear can be out of bounds. Include a halt word for safety. Ban taking card numbers or charts away. Set the hours before any live step. Name the person who may widen the scope. A chat message is not approval. A discussion with no live step is different. These rules are not an uptime credit. Number the version and cite it later. Keep a copy where the on-call person can open it.',
        'r2': "A film set keeps a stunt sheet that says which falls are allowed, which walls are real, and the medic's name in large type. A performer may attempt the marked fall and may not cut the safety line for a prettier angle. A sheet that lives only in the producer's head is how a joke becomes an injury. A chat in the trailer, with nobody falling, is a different rehearsal. The action stops when the halt word is yelled, even if the shot looked perfect. Last year's sheet still has last year's medic number, so it gets rewritten before anyone climbs. In the real activity, the stunt sheet is the rules of engagement and the medic is the on-call contact. The safety line is the forbidden zone, and a trailer chat with no fall is a tabletop.",
        'r4': "Print the emergency contact on the first page. Forbid any touch of life-safety networks. Ban removal of card numbers or clinical files. Set the hours before the first live step. Add a halt word for safety and patient care. Separate live work from a discussion-only tabletop. Number the version and cite it on the summary. Send scope additions through the named authorizer. File a copy where the on-call defender can open it. Rewrite any template that still shows another site's names.",
        'exam': 'Use this label when the stem lists permitted actions, forbidden zones, and the person to call if a system wobbles. A public-site scan that stops at the life-safety network is this boundary. Do not use it for a questionnaire about controls. Do not use it for an uptime credit. A discussion with nobody sending a payload is a tabletop. The version that bound the work matters, and a mid-test addition needs the named authorizer.',
    },
    'Service-level agreements': {
        'body': "A service-level agreement sets measurable service levels and the remedy when a level is missed. Uptime, response time, and restore time belong on that page only when someone can measure them. A memorandum of understanding records shared intent and is often less enforceable. A memorandum of agreement records cooperation and still is not the commercial bargain for money and data. The master service agreement is the master contract that holds standing legal terms. A statement of work is the specific job under that master contract. A non-disclosure agreement protects secrets and does not create a service level. A business partnership agreement divides work, risk, and duties between partners. Mixing these papers is a common trap, because a friendly memo cannot be scored like a timed response.\n\nThe agreement becomes real when someone measures it. A mail host can owe a human response within sixty minutes for a severity-one incident, and a miss creates a credit the operations lead actually logs. A percentage that nobody tracks is a slogan. Response time and restore time are different, because answering the phone is not the same as bringing the service back. Planned maintenance may be excluded, and the exclusion should be narrow enough that every outage cannot be renamed maintenance. A credit is a remedy inside the deal. It is not a full picture of harm to patients, so the credit does not close the incident by itself. The clock needs a start: ticket opened, monitoring alert, or supplier acknowledgement. A fuzzy start makes every miss arguable.\n\nThe clinic mail schedule required a severity-one response within sixty minutes of the ticket open time. Ticket INC-441 opened at 02:14. The supplier replied at 04:02, which was one hundred eight minutes, and the operations lead logged the credit the same week. The schedule sat under the master contract. The job page named which mail service the numbers covered. A draft that defined severity-one as total loss of every site worldwide was marked up, because that definition would never pay. The hours promised to the clinic's own customers were checked against this supplier's hours so the clinic was not promising a speed it could not buy. Repeated misses were written as a path to end the service, not only as a small credit.\n\nA defender assigns one owner, reviews the figures on a cadence, and writes down the misses. Availability math will not show whether a stranger can walk in, so this page does not replace a break-in test. A perfect uptime number can also hide weak access practices, so it does not replace a right to look at controls. Notice targets inside the schedule can be tighter than an old habit, and counsel still decides what must be told outside the company under the law. Renegotiate when a new product appears that the schedule never named. Students treat any vendor paper as this one. The separating fact is a measurable level plus a remedy someone actually tracks.",
        'r0': 'Define the metric, the event that starts the clock, the exclusions, and the remedy, such as a credit or an exit after repeated misses. Separate response time from restore time, and name an owner who logs misses. A clinic mail schedule required a human response within sixty minutes of ticket open for severity one. Ticket INC-441 opened at 02:14 and drew a reply at 04:02, and the one-hundred-eight-minute miss was logged as a credit. The schedule commonly attaches under the master contract, while a job page identifies the covered service. This paper is not an intent memo, a cooperation memo, a secrecy promise, or a partnership charter. Compare supplier hours with the hours you promise your own customers so the hops are added up.',
        'r1': 'A service-level agreement sets a measurable level. The level might be uptime or response time. A miss should create a tracked credit. Someone has to write the miss down. Answering a call is not restoring the service. The clock needs a clear start. A cooperation memo is a different paper. A master contract is a different paper. A job page is a different paper. A secrecy promise is a different paper. A partnership split is a different paper. An unread percentage proves nothing. Add up the hours across each supplier. Name one owner for the figures.',
        'r2': "A commuter board posts the minutes until the train and the refund rule for a late arrival. A number that no conductor writes down never produces a refund. Answering the station phone is not the same as putting the train back on the track. A friendly note between two towns about maybe sharing a platform is not the board. The deed to the station is not the board, and a promise to keep next year's map quiet is not the board. Three railroads can each add an hour, while the passenger still expects one arrival. In operations, the board is the service-level agreement and the refund is the credit for a miss. The friendly note is a memorandum, and the deed is the master contract.",
        'r4': 'Write the metric, the clock start, and the exclusions. Log each miss and claim the credit the schedule allows. Separate response time from restore time. Attach the schedule under the master contract. Reject a severity definition that can never be met. Name one owner for the monthly figures. Compare the hours you promise customers with the hours suppliers owe you. Renegotiate when a new product is not on the schedule. Keep this page off memos and secrecy slips. Review the figures on a set cadence.',
        'exam': 'A service-level agreement sets measurable service levels and a remedy when a level is missed. A memorandum of understanding records intent, and a memorandum of agreement records cooperation. Both are often less enforceable than the commercial contract. The master service agreement is the master contract. A statement of work is the specific job under it. A non-disclosure agreement protects secrets. A business partnership agreement sets the partnership. An unmeasured percentage is not proof that a level exists.',
    },
    'Memoranda of agreement and understanding': {
        'body': "People reach for a memorandum when two organizations want to cooperate without opening a full commercial negotiation. The two names are easy to swap. A memorandum of agreement is a cooperative commitment, such as sending staff to a joint exercise or sharing lessons afterward. A memorandum of understanding records a shared intent, a statement that the parties are pointed the same way and may explore a next step. Both are often less enforceable than a contract, and neither one replaces a contract when money and data duties are serious. Payment terms, liability caps, and instructions for personal data belong in the contract, not in the memo alone.\n\nTwo neighborhood clinics can plan a Saturday drill in a memorandum while any flow of patient records stays in a services contract. That contract names who decides the purposes of the records and who only follows instructions. The memorandum is the wrong only home for card numbers, clinical files, or a processor's duties. Hopes about protection can be mentioned, and those hopes still need a contract, a measurable service schedule, or a job page before anyone relies on them. A non-disclosure agreement, which limits how secrets may be repeated, is a different paper. A partnership charter that divides daily chores and risk is a different paper. The master contract and the specific job page are different again.\n\nRiverside Clinic and Harbor Clinic signed a memorandum of understanding that said they intended to run a joint Saturday drill in June and would each send two observers. The face of the memo said it recorded intent and was not the data-sharing contract. Patient-record exchange, if any drill note included a real chart, was pointed at the services contract dated the prior January. The memo carried an end date in July so it would not outlive the drill by a decade. The project plan linked to the legal file instead of to a slide that only said the clinics had agreed. When a breach question appeared later, counsel used the contract, not the memo, to see notice and liability.\n\nBefore signature, ask one question: if the data or the money matters, where is the contract? The memo can authorize people to talk, and talking is not a permitted attempt to show impact. A phrase about best efforts, with no control named, is atmosphere rather than evidence. An outside reviewer's packet and the careful daily operation after go-live keep their own names. Students treat every friendly paper as binding commercial terms. The separating fact is whether the paper records intent or a cooperative commitment, and whether money and data duties sit in a real contract beside it.",
        'r0': 'Separate a cooperation commitment, the memorandum of agreement, from a record of shared direction, the memorandum of understanding. Neither instrument is the commercial contract for payment, liability, or personal-data instructions, and both are often less enforceable. Two clinics may schedule a joint drill in the memo while a services contract governs any actual record flow, including who decides purposes and who follows instructions. The paper should say whether it is meant to bind, should carry an end date, and should point to the contract when one exists. A June drill memo that expires in July is a fitting shape. These memos are distinct from a secrecy promise, a partnership charter, a measurable service schedule, and the master contract. They do not authorize a break-in test and do not prove that controls operated.',
        'r1': 'One memo records a cooperative commitment. The other memo records shared intent. People mix the two names up. Neither memo replaces a contract for money. Neither memo replaces a contract for personal data. A drill plan can live in the memo. Real record sharing belongs in the contract. Say on the face whether it binds. Give the memo an end date. A secrecy promise is a different paper. A partnership charter is a different paper. An uptime schedule is a different paper. Best efforts, with no control named, are thin. Keep the memo in the legal file.',
        'r2': 'Two choirs exchange letters before they ever share a stage. The stronger letter promises that each choir will send singers to a joint rehearsal. The softer letter only says both choirs like the idea of someday singing together. Neither letter is the paid recording contract, the locked score cabinet, or the poster that lists ticket prices. An undated letter can hang in the hallway years after the concert was abandoned. Friendliness is not the deed. In the real paperwork, the stronger letter is a memorandum of agreement and the softer letter is a memorandum of understanding. The recording contract is the commercial agreement that has to carry money and any sharing of real records.',
        'r4': 'Name which memo you are signing, agreement or understanding. State whether the writers intend to be bound. Put payment, liability, and personal-data duties in a contract. Point the memo at that contract when one exists. Add an end date so a drill memo cannot linger. File it where the project owner can find it. Do not treat a best-efforts line as a control. Do not use the memo as permission to break in. Read it again when money or a breach appears. Keep it separate from the master contract and the job page.',
        'exam': 'A memorandum of agreement records cooperation. A memorandum of understanding records intent. Both are often less enforceable than a contract, and neither replaces the contract when money and data duties are serious. Do not call either one the master contract, the specific job, the measurable service levels, the secrecy promise, or the partnership agreement. A drill memo beside a data contract is the usual picture. If the stem turns on payment, liability, or personal data, look for the contract.',
    },
    'Master service agreements and statements of work': {
        'body': "Two papers do different jobs, and exams swap them because both sit in the same vendor folder. The master service agreement is the master contract. It holds the standing legal terms that should survive from job to job: liability, venue, how secrets are handled at a high level, and how the parties end the relationship. The statement of work is the specific job under that master contract. It holds the dates, the price for this piece of work, and the deliverables. Confidentiality language in the master terms does not, by itself, describe this month's task. A one-page description of a task does not, by itself, set liability for every future task.\n\nRead them as a pair. The master contract answers what is always true between the parties. The job page answers what is true for this engagement. If the job page is silent on a topic the master contract already settled, the master contract still speaks. If they contradict each other, someone has to name the winner in writing before work starts. A measurable service schedule often hangs under the master contract, while the job page points at which service those numbers cover. A memorandum of intent does not become the master contract because it was signed first. A secrecy promise does not become the job page because it limits gossip. A partnership charter that splits daily chores is a third kind of paper.\n\nIn May, a clinic's master contract from the prior year already covered confidentiality, liability caps, and the right to leave for a material breach. Statement of work number 17, issued this month, covered one network assessment. The window was 3 May through 14 May. The deliverable was a findings report, not a standing duty to operate the clinic network. The assessment rules, including forbidden zones and the on-call number, were attached to that job page rather than stuffed into the master contract as if every future vendor would inherit them. Finance could see the price on the job page without reopening the liability cap. When a second assessment was proposed in July, the team wrote a new job page instead of editing the May dates in place.\n\nA defender checks that the job you think you bought is the job named on the page, and that the standing terms you need are actually in the master contract. Do not let a job page quietly weaken a liability cap or a data duty. Do not let the master contract be so vague that nobody can tell whether the assessment includes a lobby visit. Students call every vendor signature a master contract, or they call the master contract a single task. The separating fact is standing legal terms versus one job's dates and deliverables.",
        'r0': 'The master service agreement is the master contract for standing legal terms such as liability, venue, confidentiality hooks, and exit. The statement of work is the specific job under it: dates, price, and deliverables. A clinic master contract covered confidentiality and liability, while statement of work 17 covered one network assessment from 3 May through 14 May whose deliverable was a findings report. A conflict between the two papers is resolved in writing before work starts. A service schedule may hang under the master contract, but the schedule is not the job page and the job page is not the master contract. A secrecy promise and a cooperation memo do not take either role. The next engagement gets a new job page rather than a silent edit of the old dates.',
        'r1': 'The master contract holds the standing legal terms. The job page holds this task. Dates and deliverables live on the job page. Liability and exit live in the master contract. Read the two papers as a pair. Write down a winner if they conflict. A secrecy slip is neither paper. A cooperation memo is neither paper. A service schedule is not the job page. Do not hide a lobby visit in vague master language. Give the next job its own page. Do not weaken a data duty from the job page. Finance can price the job without reopening liability.',
        'r2': 'A building lease sets the standing rules for every tenant: rent method, insurance, and how either side can leave. A work order this month says which rooms will be painted, on which days, for which fee. The lease does not name the paint color, and the work order does not rewrite the insurance clause. A note that says two neighbors might share a ladder someday is not the lease. A promise not to describe the alarm to strangers is not the work order. If the work order and the lease disagree about who pays for damage, someone marks the winner before the brushes come out. In the vendor folder, the lease is the master service agreement and the work order is the statement of work. Paint dates are this job, and insurance is a standing term.',
        'r4': 'Identify the master contract and the current job page. Confirm liability, venue, and exit sit in the master contract. Confirm dates, price, and deliverables sit on the job page. Resolve conflicts in writing before work starts. Attach test boundaries to the job they govern. Do not let a job page weaken a data duty. Write a new job page for the next engagement. Keep cooperation memos and secrecy slips in their own files. Show finance the job price without reopening the liability cap. Check that a lobby visit is named if you expect one.',
        'exam': 'The master service agreement is the master contract. The statement of work is the specific job under it, with dates and deliverables. Do not swap them. A memorandum records intent or cooperation and is often less enforceable. A service-level agreement sets measurable service levels. A non-disclosure agreement protects secrets. A business partnership agreement sets the partnership. A findings report for one May assessment belongs on the job page, not in the standing liability clause.',
    },
    'Non-disclosure agreements': {
        'body': "A non-disclosure agreement limits how the other side may repeat what they learn. The paper protects secrets. The signature does not, by itself, make the other party's security any good, and the signature is not a control you can operate. A vendor can sign on Monday and still have to meet an encryption standard before any data moves. The secrecy promise does not set a measurable uptime level, does not describe one job's dates, and does not divide partnership chores. A cooperation memo is a different paper again. People treat the signature as if the security program were finished. The program is a separate pile of evidence: encryption, logging, access removal, and a current outside report.\n\nThe text names what counts as confidential, who may see it, how long the duty lasts, and what happens if the duty is broken. It should also name what is already public, what the receiver already knew, and what a court or a regulator can force into the open. A one-way promise binds only the recipient. A mutual promise binds both sides, which is the right shape when diagrams flow one way and pricing flows the other. A clinic that hands network diagrams to an assessor needs the assessor bound. A vendor that learns patient volumes needs the vendor bound. A template copied from another deal, with the wrong party in the first paragraph, binds nobody you care about.\n\nIn January a hosting vendor signed a mutual secrecy paper before a sales workshop. The workshop slides still claimed encryption on every path. The contract exhibit for an old file transfer showed clear text. The January signature did not close that gap. The security lead wrote the encryption requirement into the master contract, including stored files protected with AES-256 and a ban on keeping card security codes after authorization. The secrecy file stayed in the folder because the workshop notes were sensitive, and the file tab said secrecy rather than control evidence. When an engineer later offered another customer's configuration as a sample, the secrecy paper was a reason to refuse the dump.\n\nA defender checks that the named parties are the people who will actually learn something, that the duty lasts as long as the secret matters, and that nobody is using the signature as a substitute for a control. Return or destruction of notes at the end of the talks belongs on the page if copies matter. A broken secrecy duty is a contract problem of its own. Students select this paper whenever any vendor has signed anything. The fact that separates it from the neighbors is a limit on sharing secrets, not proof that encryption, logging, or access control exists.",
        'r0': "A non-disclosure agreement defines confidential material, permitted recipients, duration, exceptions for public or compelled disclosure, and the consequence of a leak. Mutual terms fit when information will flow both ways, and a one-way term fits when only one side will learn something new. A January signature from a hosting vendor did not satisfy the separate encryption standard, which still required AES-256 for stored clinic files and a ban on retaining card security codes after authorization. The secrecy paper can justify refusing another customer's configuration dump. It does not set a sixty-minute response level, does not name one job's dates, and does not allocate partnership patch duties. Return or destruction of notes is part of the closeout when copies matter.",
        'r1': "A non-disclosure agreement protects secrets. The signature is not a security program. The vendor must still meet the encryption bar. Name who may see the secret. Name how long the duty lasts. Say what a court can force open. Use a mutual promise when both sides learn. A copied template can name the wrong party. Keep the secrecy file separate from control evidence. Refuse another customer's sample dump. This paper does not set uptime. This paper does not describe one job. Partnership chores belong in a different paper.",
        'r2': "Two cooks trade recipes in a back hallway and promise not to post the cards on the diner window. The promise stops gossip. The promise does not lock the walk-in fridge, and it does not make the fridge cold. A handshake that the fridge is probably fine is a different conversation. A lease for the whole diner is a different document, and this week's catering order is a different document again. If one cook later offers a third diner's recipe book as a sample, the hallway promise is a reason to say no. Back in the vendor workshop, the hallway promise is the non-disclosure agreement and the fridge lock is the encryption control. The signature keeps secrets from spreading, and it does not prove the control exists.",
        'r4': "Confirm the named parties will actually receive secrets. Set how long the secrecy duty lasts. List what is already public and what a court can compel. Choose a mutual promise when information flows both ways. File the paper as secrecy, not as control evidence. Write encryption duties into the contract beside it. Refuse dumps of other customers' configurations. Require return or destruction of notes when the talks end. Do not treat the signature as proof of logging or access control. Reread the first paragraph of any copied template.",
        'exam': "A non-disclosure agreement protects secrets by limiting how they may be shared. The signature does not make the vendor's security good, and the vendor must still meet the encryption standard. Do not confuse it with measurable service levels, the master contract, one job's dates, a cooperation memo, or a partnership agreement. A clear-text exhibit still matters after the secrecy paper is signed. Refusing another customer's files is consistent with the paper. Treating the signature as the control is the trap.",
    },
    'Business partnership agreements': {
        'body': "A business partnership agreement sets how two parties share work, risk, and duties. The paper is the partnership agreement. Security chores count only when someone writes them in: who patches a shared system, who may administer it, and who tells the other partner about an incident. A memorandum can record that two clinics hope to cooperate, and that memo is often less enforceable than this agreement. The master contract can hold standing terms with a vendor who is not the partner. A statement of work can name one purchased job. A non-disclosure agreement can protect secrets. A service-level agreement can set a measurable clock with a credit. None of those papers is the partnership split unless the partnership text says so.\n\nThe allocation has to be something you can check. Each duty names an owner, a clock, and the evidence that the duty happened. A shared system needs a patch owner and a backup owner for holidays. Incident notice between the partners needs a clock at least as tight as the clocks each partner owes to customers or regulators. Joiners and leavers on both sides need a path off the shared admin list. Money, liability between the partners, and how the partnership ends belong in the same paper or in a paper it clearly points to. A slide that says both teams care is not an allocation anyone can audit.\n\nHarbor Clinic and Riverside Clinic ran a joint imaging program under a partnership agreement. Harbor's team had to patch the shared imaging servers within fourteen days of a critical fix. Riverside's privacy officer had to notify Harbor within twenty-four hours of a confirmed incident on that system. A critical fix released on 3 April showed a Harbor change ticket closed on 9 April, inside the fourteen days. A misdirected image confirmed at 11:00 on 2 June produced a notice to Harbor timestamped at 15:40 the same day. The quarterly review checked the change ticket and the notice time, not the signature page. A Saturday drill memo stayed in another folder so nobody would cite it as the patch duty.\n\nA defender samples the evidence on a calendar. A duty with no owner is a gap to write down before an incident assigns it during a panic. A new shared system needs the paper updated before data moves onto it. Students call every joint project a memorandum, or they assume a vendor master contract covers the partner clinic. The fact that separates this paper is a partnership allocation of work, risk, and duties, including the security chores that were actually written down.",
        'r0': 'A business partnership agreement allocates work, risk, and duties between partners, and security duties exist only when the text names them. One joint imaging program gave Harbor fourteen days to install a critical patch and gave Riverside twenty-four hours to notify Harbor after a confirmed incident on the shared system. The 3 April fix closed on a 9 April change ticket, and the 2 June notice left at 15:40 after an 11:00 confirmation. Evidence is the ticket and the timestamp, not a slide about shared concern. A cooperation memo, a vendor master contract, a single job page, a secrecy promise, and a measured service credit do not become this allocation by sitting in the same cabinet. Update the allocation before a new shared system receives data.',
        'r1': 'A partnership agreement splits work, risk, and duties. Security chores count only if they are written. Name who patches the shared system. Name who reports an incident to the partner. Give each duty an owner and a clock. Check tickets, not the signature alone. A drill memo is a different paper. A vendor master contract is a different paper. A secrecy promise is a different paper. A measured uptime credit is a different paper. Add a new shared system to the paper first. A slide about caring is not a duty. Sample the evidence every quarter.',
        'r2': 'Two bakeries share one oven in a side room and write down who cleans it, who fixes the thermostat, and who calls the other bakery if smoke appears. The note is useful only if a person and a time sit next to each chore. A friendly postcard about maybe baking together someday does not name the thermostat. A lease with the flour company does not decide which bakery calls during a fire. A promise not to publish the oven recipe does not decide who cleans. In the clinic program, the oven note is the business partnership agreement and the thermostat chore is the patch duty. The smoke call is the incident notice between the partners, and the postcard is only a memorandum.',
        'r4': 'Read who owns each security chore in the partnership paper. Confirm patch timing and incident notice are both written. Sample change tickets against the patch clock. Sample notice timestamps against the incident clock. Keep drill memos from being cited as the duty. Update the paper before a new shared system goes live. Name a backup owner for holidays. Put joiners and leavers on a shared access review. Do not assume a vendor contract covers the partner. File the quarterly sample with the agreement.',
        'exam': 'A business partnership agreement is the partnership paper: shared work, shared risk, and shared duties, including security chores only when they are written in. Do not call a memorandum the same thing. A memorandum records intent or cooperation and is often less enforceable. The master contract, the specific job, the secrecy promise, and the measurable service levels are neighboring papers. The trap is a joint project with no named owner for patching or incident notice. Tickets and timestamps are the evidence, not a slide.',
    },
    'Internal and external compliance reporting': {
        'body': "Two reports can leave the same incident, and they do not share an audience. Internal reporting tells leadership what is actually known, including what is still uncertain, on a clock the organization sets for itself. External reporting tells a regulator, a customer, or a partner what a rule or a contract requires, and it leaves on that outer clock. Counsel usually decides what the outer notice must say and when it is due. Leadership still receives the internal facts the same day the clock starts, even while the external wording is unfinished. A penalty, a lost processing privilege, and a loss of trust may follow later. Those outcomes are not the report.\n\nThe internal note names what happened, which systems, which kinds of data, what is still unknown, and who owns the next fact. The note does not wait for a perfect story. The external notice follows the duty that applies, which might be a statute, a contract clause, or a partner rule. Those clocks can differ, so a customer clause and a regulator rule should not be forced into one template. What you tell the public, if you tell the public at all, is a further decision. The internal channel is not a press release. A press release is not a filing to a regulator. Mixing the channels sends speculation to the wrong reader or hides facts from your own leaders.\n\nOn Tuesday at 09:00 a clinic privacy lead opened a clock after a misdirected file of patient names was confirmed. By 16:30 the same day the executive channel had the facts: about four hundred names, one recipient outside the clinic, the file retrieved, and an open question about whether the recipient had copied it. Counsel had not yet sent an external notice. The customer contract's seventy-two-hour timer ran on its own line. The question of a regulator filing stayed open. The internal note was marked internal so it would not be forwarded as if it were the external letter. The next morning the facts were updated rather than replaced with a softer story.\n\nA defender keeps both clocks visible, names the owner of each notice, and preserves the draft history. A later reader should be able to see what leadership knew and when. Sending internal speculation to a regulator is a mistake. Waiting to brief leadership until the external letter is polished is also a mistake. Students collapse both reports into a single announcement. The fact that separates them is the audience and the rule that sets the time: leaders now, and outsiders when a rule or a contract says they must be told.",
        'r0': "Internal reporting gives leadership the known facts, the unknowns, and an owner for the next fact, and it should land the same day the clock starts. External reporting tells a regulator, a customer, or a partner what an applicable rule requires, on that rule's clock, with counsel choosing the content. A Tuesday 09:00 confirmation of a misdirected file reached executives at 16:30 with a count of about four hundred names, while the external letter was still unsent. A seventy-two-hour customer clause and a separate regulator question must not share one template. Preserve draft history so the internal facts are not later rewritten into a softer story. Penalties, lost privileges, and trust loss are later consequences, not the report itself.",
        'r1': 'Tell your own leaders the facts. Include what you still do not know. Do that on the day the clock starts. Tell outsiders only when a rule requires it. A customer clock and a regulator clock can differ. Counsel chooses the external wording. Do not forward the internal note as the filing. Do not wait for perfect words before briefing leaders. A press release is a third decision. Mark the internal note so it stays internal. Keep the draft history. A penalty is not the report. A lost privilege is not the report.',
        'r2': "A head cook tells the owner the same afternoon that a tray of food left with the wrong delivery driver, including what is still unknown about whether the driver ate any. A call to the health board goes out only if the rule for that kind of mistake requires the call, and the board call uses the board's questions. The owner does not receive a polished silence until the board letter is perfect. The board does not receive the owner's private guesswork labeled as a filing. A newspaper note, if any, is yet another conversation. In the incident, the owner's briefing is internal reporting and the board call is external reporting. The same-day briefing and the rule-driven notice are both required, and they are not the same document.",
        'r4': 'Open the internal brief the day the clock starts. Include known facts, unknowns, and the next owner. Mark that brief so it is not forwarded as a filing. Ask counsel which external notices are due. Keep each external clock on its own line. Do not send speculation to a regulator. Update leaders when facts change instead of softening the first note. Save the draft history with times. Separate any public statement from the regulatory letter. Record send times next to each duty.',
        'exam': 'Internal reporting tells leadership the truth, including uncertainty, and the same-day brief is the usual picture. External reporting tells a regulator, a customer, or a partner what a rule requires, on time, with counsel on the content. Do not merge them into one announcement. A penalty, a lost license, and lost trust are consequences, not the report. A customer clock such as seventy-two hours can run beside a different regulator question. Forwarding the internal note as the filing is the trap.',
    },
    'Fines and sanctions': {
        'body': "Fines and sanctions are penalties a regulator or a court can impose for breaking a rule. The penalty is a consequence. The reason to protect people is the duty to those people, and the penalty is not that reason. A missed notification deadline can bring a monetary penalty. A serious failure can also bring orders that restrict how an activity is run. Those orders are still not the same event as losing the legal ability to operate or to take a kind of payment. A contract credit is a different consequence. Patients leaving because trust broke is a different consequence again. Naming only the money teaches the wrong lesson.\n\nTreat the deadline as a clock you can see. Write the date the duty started, the date the notice left, and the owner. If notice was due on a Friday and left the following Tuesday, the lateness is a fact, not a mood. Hoping the penalty will be small does not protect the records. Paying a penalty does not restore a file that was never safeguarded, and paying does not erase a notice you still owe. A public apology is not the penalty. The penalty is the consequence an authority imposes for the broken rule. Under-reporting to look smaller can become a second broken rule.\n\nA clinic notified a regulator four days after the deadline that applied to a misdirected file. The miss, the deadline date, and the send date went into the board pack beside the harm to the people named in the file. The privacy lead kept finishing the notice content and the containment work. Finance reserved an amount only after counsel described a realistic range, and that reserve was not treated as a public admission. The board minute separated penalty exposure from the operational repairs. Nobody wrote that a modest penalty would mean a modest incident. The people in the file remained the point of the work.\n\nA defender tracks the clocks that can create a penalty and keeps evidence of what was done on time. Fear of a penalty should not distort the facts told to leadership. A small expected penalty is not a reason to skip the external notice or the internal brief. Students answer every consequence question with a fine. The fact that separates this consequence is an authority's penalty for a broken rule, as distinct from trust leaving, a contract credit, or a processor cutting off card payments.",
        'r0': "Fines and sanctions are penalties imposed by a regulator or a court for a broken rule, and they are a consequence rather than the purpose of the duty. A clinic notice that left four days after the applicable deadline was recorded with the deadline date, the send date, and an owner. Orders that restrict an activity can accompany a monetary penalty and still differ from loss of a license or of card processing. A contract credit and a loss of trust stay on separate lines of the board pack. A finance reserve based on counsel's range is not a public admission and does not replace containment or the notice still owed. Under-reporting to shrink the look of the event can become a further violation.",
        'r1': 'A fine is a penalty for a broken rule. The duty to people is larger than the penalty. A late notice can bring that penalty. Write the due date and the send date. Paying does not restore unprotected records. Paying does not cancel a notice still owed. An order that limits an activity can be separate. Losing the right to operate is a different event. A contract credit is a different event. People leaving is a different event. Do not shrink the story to shrink the penalty. Keep the operational work moving. Brief leaders on the exposure and the harm.',
        'r2': 'A parking ticket on the windshield prices a broken parking rule. The ticket is not the reason the brakes work, and the ticket is not the crash. Paying the ticket does not repair a bumper and does not excuse a second ticket tomorrow. A city order that closes the garage entirely is a harsher event than the ticket on the glass. A refund owed to a rider under a private charter is a different piece of paper. Neighbors who stop trusting the driver are not the ticket either. In a compliance matter, the ticket is the fine or sanction and the brakes are the duty to protect people. Closing the garage is closer to losing a license, and the rider refund is a contractual remedy.',
        'r4': 'Record the deadline date, the send date, and the owner. Tell leadership the lateness as a fact. Keep containment and notice work moving after a miss. Ask counsel about the realistic penalty range before finance reserves money. Do not describe a reserve as a public admission. Separate the penalty from lost trust and from contract credits. Separate the penalty from a suspended processing privilege. Do not under-report to make the event look smaller. Keep evidence of steps that were on time. Revisit the clock that created the miss.',
        'exam': 'Fines and sanctions are penalties a regulator or a court imposes for breaking a rule. The penalty follows a broken rule and is not why people deserve protection. A missed notice deadline can create that penalty, and the dates belong in the answer. Do not call lost trust a fine. Do not call a contract credit a fine. Do not call a processor cutoff, or a lost license, a fine. Paying the penalty does not finish the notice or the repair. Under-reporting to look smaller is its own trap.',
    },
    'Reputational damage': {
        'body': 'Reputational damage is the loss of trust, not a fine. A fine is a penalty a regulator or a partner imposes for breaking a rule. Trust can fail when that penalty is small, or when there is no penalty at all, because patients, customers, or staff decide to go elsewhere. The harm shows up as empty appointments, lost contracts, and hard questions from the board. It does not show up as a line on a penalty notice. People feel it after they learn the organization was careless with records they had handed over.\n\nThe path is public knowledge plus a judgment about care. People hear about a breach, a lost laptop, or a vendor incident, and they ask whether you noticed, whether you told them in time, and whether you treated their records as your own problem. A late or muddy notice can cost more trust than the original mistake. Telling leadership the facts is one duty. Telling a regulator, a customer, or a partner what a rule requires, on time, is another. Those reports can be done well and people can still leave. Paying the penalty does not restore the trust. The trust loss is its own consequence.\n\nAfter a public notice on 2 January, the clinic penalty for a late patient letter was 5,000 dollars. In the next 30 days, 40 households asked to move their records to another practice. The front desk logged each request with the reason trust after the breach notice. The records themselves had been recovered. The March appointment count sat below the January count. Leadership had the internal facts on the same day as the notice. Counsel sent the external letter the rule required. Neither correct step stopped the households from leaving. The small penalty and the empty chairs were not the same event.\n\nPlan the notice so it is plain, on time, and matched to what happened, because confusion feeds the loss of trust. Track the signals that show trust moving, including cancellations, contract questions, press calls, and staff resignations. Write them beside the incident, not inside the penalty file. Do not describe the penalty as if it measured the trust loss. Brief the people who answer the phone so they do not invent a worse story. After the notice, show what changed, such as a control that would have stopped this path, without promising that no later mistake can occur. Students pick the fine whenever money appears in the stem. Facts told to leaders, and notices a rule requires, are reporting duties. The separating fact is the loss of trust. Households can leave even when the penalty was small.',
        'r0': 'Separate the trust loss from any penalty amount. After the 2 January public notice, the late letter penalty was 5,000 dollars. Within 30 days, 40 households requested record transfers, and the desk coded each request with the reason trust after the breach notice. The March appointment count dropped against January even though the records had been recovered. Leadership had the internal facts the same day. Counsel sent the external notice the rule required. Neither step is the trust loss, and paying 5,000 does not restore it. Track cancellations, contract questions, and press calls beside the incident. Do not enter the penalty in the books as if the number measured trust.',
        'r1': 'This harm is lost trust. It is not a fine. People can leave when the penalty is small. A public breach can empty the appointment book. A clear notice still may not keep them. A muddy notice can cost more trust. Telling leaders the facts is a different duty. Telling outsiders on time is a different duty. Track cancellations beside the incident. Do not file the penalty as if it were the trust loss. Brief the people who answer the phone. They should not invent a worse story. Show what changed after the notice. Do not promise that no future mistake can happen.',
        'r2': 'A small kitchen serves a full room until a bad night shows up in the local paper. The city fee for a late note was small. Diners still left, and the next week had empty chairs. The fee did not bring the diners back. Telling the staff the truth at dawn, and telling the city what the rule required, were both done. People left anyway because they no longer trusted the pots. A reporter asking about the sauce is not the same event as the bill from the city. In the real harm, the empty chairs are the people who stop trusting you, and the small city fee is the fine. The fine can be paid while the trust stays gone.',
        'r4': 'Describe the trust loss separately from any penalty amount. Log cancellations, transfers, and press calls beside the incident. Brief the phone staff with the facts you can support. Do not invent a worse story. Do not edit the public notice to hide the event. Do not book the penalty as if it measured trust. Tell leaders the internal facts. Send external notices the rule requires, on time. Write down what changed after the event. Review the trust signals again after 30 days. Keep the penalty file and the trust notes as two records.',
        'exam': 'Choose this when people lose trust and leave, even if a penalty was small or absent. Do not choose it for the penalty itself. Fines and sanctions are imposed for breaking a rule, and they are a different harm. Do not choose this merely because leaders were told the facts or because a required notice went to a regulator. Those reports can be correct while trust still falls. Empty appointments and departed households are the clues. Paying the penalty does not close the trust loss.',
    },
    'Loss of license': {
        'body': "Loss of license means someone with authority can stop you from operating, or can stop you from taking a kind of payment. A regulator can remove a license the activity requires. A partner can remove a privilege the activity requires. A card processor can cut off card processing when card-data rules are ignored, even in a week when no court has set a fine. The activity stops. Paying a penalty and continuing is a different outcome. A contract credit for a slow response is a different outcome. Whether people still trust the name is a different outcome again. The visible sign is a door that no longer opens for that activity.\n\nThe mechanism is an authority you already accepted. Processor rules say what card data may be stored, how fast a problem must be reported, and what the processor may do if the rules are ignored. A health authority's rules say what must remain true before patients can keep being seen. The practical artifact is a letter or a portal status that moves from active to suspended, plus failed transactions at the desk. A warning that does not yet stop the activity is earlier, and the warning is not yet the loss. Reinstatement, when it exists, is a list of proof the authority named, not a press statement.\n\nA clinic's card processor suspended the merchant account after an assessment found security codes still stored after authorization, with no dated plan to delete them. By Friday the front desk could not take cards. The appointment staff started a workaround that counsel had to review before anyone used it. No fine had been calculated. The contract with a referring hospital was still in force. The loss that stopped the week was the processing privilege. The repair list had to satisfy the processor, including evidence that security codes were no longer retained, before the merchant status could return to active. Declined cards at the desk were the fact staff could see.\n\nA defender puts reinstatement conditions on a work list with evidence beside each line. A single path for a critical activity, whether a license or a processor, belongs on the risk register before it fails. Do not assume a monetary penalty is the worst line on the page. Students pick a fine whenever money appears, and they pick reputation whenever patients are upset. The fact that separates this outcome is an authority shutting the door on an activity, not merely pricing a mistake.",
        'r0': "Loss of license is the removal, by a regulator or a partner, of the ability to operate or to take a kind of payment. A card processor can suspend a merchant account when card-data rules are ignored, such as security codes retained after authorization. In one clinic the suspension hit before any fine was calculated, and Friday's card transactions failed at the desk. Reinstatement depended on evidence that those codes were no longer stored, not on a public statement. A warning letter that leaves the activity running is not yet this loss. A contract credit and a trust problem remain different consequences and can exist in the same week without closing the door.",
        'r1': 'An authority can shut down an activity. A regulator can take a license away. A partner can take a payment privilege away. A card processor can stop card payments. Staff then see declined cards at the desk. A fine you can pay is a different outcome. A contract credit is a different outcome. Lost trust is a different outcome. A warning that leaves you open is earlier. Reinstatement needs the proof the authority named. Put that proof on a work list. A single path for payments belongs on the risk list. Do not treat a press note as reinstatement.',
        'r2': "A health inspector can close a kitchen, not merely hand the cook a ticket. Once the sign is on the door, service stops even if nobody has calculated a money penalty yet. A voucher owed to a catering client under a private deal is a different problem, and neighbors who grumble are a different problem again. A warning visit that lets the kitchen stay open is not the closing. Reopening happens only when the inspector's list is met, such as a fridge that now holds the right temperature with a log to prove it. In the payment world, the closed kitchen is a lost license or a cut-off processor. Declined cards are the sign on the door, and a fine would have been only the ticket.",
        'r4': "Identify which license or payment privilege the activity depends on. Read the rules that allow that authority to suspend you. Record the status change and the time transactions start failing. Separate the suspension from any fine still uncalculated. Build a reinstatement list from the authority's conditions. Attach evidence to each condition, including deleted security codes if that was the finding. Do not invent a public statement as a substitute for that evidence. Put single-path privileges on the risk register. Keep contract credits on a different line. Review workarounds with counsel before staff use them.",
        'exam': "Loss of license means a regulator or a partner can stop an activity or stop a kind of payment. A processor cutting off card processing is this outcome even when no fine has been set. A fine you can pay and keep operating is a different consequence. A contract credit is a different consequence. Lost trust is a different consequence. A warning that leaves the activity open is not yet the loss. Reinstatement follows the authority's proof list. Declined payments are the visible fact.",
    },
    'Contractual impacts': {
        'body': "Contractual impacts are the duties and the remedies written in your contracts. Notice to the other party, service credits, and a right to walk away are the usual forms. A signed clock can beat the team's old habit. A customer agreement that requires notice within seventy-two hours will not relax because the team used to take thirty days. The contract clock can also be tighter or looser than a regulator's clock, and counsel keeps those clocks apart. A missed contract notice is not automatically a fine. A fine is not automatically a customer's right to leave. The consequence you face is the consequence the clause names.\n\nThe mechanism is the sentence you signed. Find the notice window, the event that starts the window, the person who must send the notice, and the remedy if the notice is late. Credits follow a service schedule when a measured level is missed. A right to leave follows a breach clause, sometimes after a chance to fix the problem. None of those remedies appear because someone feels that they should. They appear because the agreement says so. A memorandum of intent usually does not carry them, because that memo records intent and is often less enforceable. Look in the master contract, the job page, and the service schedule, and use the schedule when the remedy is a credit.\n\nA regional hospital's contract required notice of a confirmed patient-data exposure within seventy-two hours of confirmation. The clinic playbook still said thirty days. The privacy lead put the seventy-two-hour clock on the incident board in the hour the exposure was confirmed, and the draft went to counsel that afternoon. The mail host's credit for a slow severity-one response stayed on a different line of the board. Credit math was not used to decide whether the hospital had to be told. When the notice went out inside the window, the file still held the clause number, the confirmation time, and the send time. Those three facts are what a later argument needs.\n\nA defender builds the incident board from the contracts rather than from memory. Compare each customer clock with the speed you can actually meet, and do that comparison before signature. After a miss, record the credit or the exit right the other party now holds. Students call every deadline a regulatory penalty. The fact that separates contractual impacts is a duty or a remedy that comes from the agreement: notice, a credit, or a right to leave.",
        'r0': 'Contractual impacts are notice duties, credits, and exit rights that the agreement itself creates, and they can be stricter than local habit. A hospital contract required notice within seventy-two hours of confirmation, against a clinic playbook that still said thirty days. The clock should be placed on the incident board in the confirmation hour, with counsel receiving the draft the same day. Clause number, confirmation time, and send time are the facts a later dispute needs. A service credit for a missed sixty-minute response stays on a separate line and does not decide whether notice is due. A memorandum of intent usually lacks these remedies. A regulatory penalty is a different consequence even when both clocks run together.',
        'r1': "A signed clock can beat the team's old habit. Read the notice window and what starts it. Seventy-two hours will not become thirty days. Name who must send the notice. A credit follows a missed measured level. A right to leave follows the breach clause. Those remedies exist only if the text says so. A friendly memo usually does not carry them. Keep a regulator clock on a separate line. Do not use credit math to skip a notice. Record the clause, the start, and the send time. Compare clocks before you sign. After a miss, record the remedy the other party holds.",
        'r2': "A catering contract says the hall must be told within a short, written time if a delivery will miss the reception, and it says the hall may cancel if the miss is serious. The cook's old habit of calling next week does not rewrite the contract. A city ticket for a different food rule is not the cancellation right. A note between two cooks about hoping to share a kitchen someday usually cannot be enforced like the catering contract. The refund line for a cold dish is yet another sentence, useful only if someone measures the dish. In the clinic file, the catering contract is the customer agreement and the short call is the contractual notice. The city ticket is a penalty from an authority, not a term you negotiated.",
        'r4': 'List every customer notice window before an incident. Write the event that starts each window. Put the tightest applicable clock on the board at confirmation. Send the draft to counsel the same day. Keep regulatory deadlines on a separate line. Keep service credits off the notice decision. Record the clause, the start time, and the send time. After a miss, write down the credit or exit right now held. Compare promised clocks with real speed before signature. Do not let an old playbook outrank the contract.',
        'exam': "Contractual impacts are duties and remedies in the contract: notice, credits, or a right to walk away. A seventy-two-hour notice that beats a thirty-day habit is the usual picture. The contract can be stricter than habit and different from a regulator's clock. Do not call the notice a fine. Do not call a fine a contractual exit. A memorandum of intent is often less enforceable and usually lacks these remedies. Measurable service credits are one contractual remedy, not the whole topic. The clause, the start, and the send time are the facts.",
    },
    'Due diligence and due care': {
        'body': 'Due diligence is the investigation before you commit. Due care is careful work that continues after the commitment is real. The investigation reads outside reports, compares candidates, maps who sits upstream, and chases contradictions in what a vendor claims. The ongoing operation patches systems, removes stale access, watches the vendor, trains people, and handles incidents on a clock. Each duty can be present or missing on its own. A strong review followed by a year of missed patches is a failure of due care. Tidy daily habits on a supplier that nobody examined before the data moved are a failure of due diligence. Swapping the two names is the usual exam mistake.\n\nA real purchase shows the sequence. Before signature, the team scores encryption, logging, exit terms, and a current outside report, and it rejects a design that keeps card security codes after authorization. That scoring is the investigation. After go-live, the same organization runs the patch cycle, reviews which vendor staff still hold admin rights, and checks whether people report suspicious messages. That cycle is the ongoing care. A new subcontractor or a new kind of data requires a fresh investigation tied to the new commitment. The fresh look does not replace the care already owed on the live system. Evidence has to be labeled by phase or the two duties blur into one story.\n\nIn March a clinic compared two hosting vendors, read the exceptions pages, and rejected the cheaper host because administrator actions were not logged. The contract with the host that met the floor was signed in April. The May patch window completed, and a June review removed two vendor accounts for people who had left. The March folder was filed as due diligence. The May patch record and the June access review were filed as due care. A later request for proof of care could not be answered with the March score sheet. A later request for proof that the host was examined before the data moved could not be answered with the June review.\n\nA defender labels evidence by phase and refuses to let a signature on an acknowledgement stand in for either duty. A signed form says someone claimed to understand a rule. The form does not show the pre-commitment investigation, and the form does not show the patches. Fines, lost privileges, and contract credits are consequences of failure, not the careful work itself. The fact that separates the two duties is the moment of commitment: investigation before that moment, careful operation after it.',
        'r0': 'Due diligence is the investigation before commitment, and due care is the ongoing careful operation after commitment. Pre-signature work includes reading exceptions, comparing candidates, mapping upstream parties, and rejecting designs that retain card security codes after authorization. Post-go-live work includes patching, access removal, vendor watch, and incident clocks. A March score sheet that rejected an unlogged admin design, an April signature, a May patch window, and a June removal of two stale vendor accounts illustrate the two phases in order. A strong March folder plus a missed May patch is a due-care failure. Tidy June operations on a host never examined before the data moved would be a due-diligence failure. A new subcontractor triggers a fresh investigation and does not erase the care still owed.',
        'r1': 'Due diligence happens before you commit. Due care happens after you commit. Read reports and compare vendors first. Patch and remove stale access later. One side can fail while the other holds. A careful review plus missed patches is a care failure. Tidy work on an unexamined vendor is a diligence failure. Label the March folder as the investigation. Label the patch record as the later care. A new subcontractor needs a fresh look. That fresh look does not replace live patching. A signed acknowledgement is neither duty. Fines are consequences, not the careful work.',
        'r2': "A family inspects the roof, the locks, and the heating before buying a house, and they write down what the inspector found. After they move in, someone still has to patch the roof each season and change the locks when a sitter leaves. A beautiful inspection followed by a leaking year is a failure of living there carefully. A tidy household in a house nobody inspected is a failure of the look before the purchase. The inspection report cannot answer a question about last month's leak. Last month's repair cannot answer whether the house was examined before the keys changed hands. In the vendor decision, the inspection is due diligence and the seasonal patching is due care. Commitment is the purchase, and the two duties sit on opposite sides of that moment.",
        'r4': "Separate the pre-signature folder from the live operating records. File vendor comparisons and exception reads as due diligence. File patch windows and access reviews as due care. Reject designs that keep card security codes before you sign. Do not answer a care question with the old score sheet. Do not answer a diligence question with this quarter's patches. Open a fresh investigation when a subcontractor is added. Keep patching the live system while that new look runs. Do not accept a signed acknowledgement as either proof. Note which duty failed when one side is strong.",
        'exam': 'Due diligence is the investigation before you commit. Due care is careful work that continues after the commitment is real. A strong review followed by missed patches is a due-care failure. Careful daily work on a vendor nobody examined is a due-diligence failure. Do not swap the two. A signed acknowledgement is neither duty. Fines, lost licenses, and contract credits are consequences, not the investigation and not the daily care. Label evidence by phase, because the March folder and the June review answer different questions.',
    },
    'Attestation and acknowledgement': {
        'body': 'Attestation and acknowledgement are formal statements that a person or a vendor understands a rule. A signature, a tick on a roster, or an annual click records that statement. It does not prove the person follows the rule. Behavior still has to be measured. Staff can sign a page about reporting suspicious mail and still click the next lookalike. A vendor can state that encryption is everywhere while an exhibit shows a cleartext transfer. The signed line is the claim. Later evidence is the check.\n\nTie the statement to a named person or a named company, and date it. The statement should name the rule and the role of the signer. An anonymous tick on a slide is weaker, because nobody can tell who claimed to understand. For staff, the usual cycle is annual. Each person acknowledges the procedure, and the team keeps a roster of who has not finished. For a vendor, the statement may sit at the bottom of a form or in a letter beside a report. The next step is the same in both cases. Compare the statement with a behavior you can count, or with a document that can disagree with it.\n\nOn 2 March, 210 clinic staff acknowledged the procedure for reporting suspicious mail. The roster showed 210 signatures and no blanks. In April an awareness exercise sent a harmless payroll lookalike. Of those 210 people, 61 used the report button and 22 clicked the link. The people who reported it were thanked. The acknowledgement file was not edited to pretend the others had behaved as the signature implied. Ticket 552 recorded the gap as 210 acknowledgements on 2 March against 61 reports in the April exercise, so the signature was not the behavior.\n\nFile the roster, then measure the behavior the rule actually requires. Do not treat a vendor signature as a control by itself. If the statement says encryption is universal, read the exhibit and the outside report before you believe it. When names are missing from the roster, chase those people. When people sign and still miss the behavior, teach again and look at the procedure. A confusing report button will produce a clean roster and a weak result. Do not alter the old signatures so they match a later hope. The historical roster stays as it was. The new count sits beside it. Students stop at the signature and call the control finished. A data subject is the person a record describes, which is not this signed statement. A duty to delete, where the law requires deletion, is not an acknowledgement. A list of what you hold and how long you keep it is not a signature either. The separating fact is the formal statement of understanding, and the fact that the statement is not proof of the behavior.',
        'r0': 'Record a dated statement that a named person or vendor understands a named rule, then measure behavior apart from that statement. On 2 March, 210 staff acknowledged the suspicious mail procedure and the roster showed zero blanks. The April exercise sent a harmless payroll lookalike. Of those 210, 61 used the report button and 22 clicked. Ticket 552 stored both figures and did not rewrite the March roster. A vendor statement from the same season is checked against exhibits. A claim of universal encryption fails if a transfer is still cleartext. The statement phase ends when the roster is complete. The behavior phase is the count, and the two phases can disagree. An annual acknowledgement without that count is only the claim.',
        'r1': 'People formally say they understand a rule. Vendors can say the same in a letter. A signature records the statement. A signature does not prove the behavior. Name the rule, the date, and the role. Keep a roster of who has not signed. Then measure what people actually do. A clean roster can hide weak reporting. Compare the statement with a count you can show. A vendor claim can still disagree with an exhibit. Do not edit old signatures to match a hope. Teach again when behavior misses the rule. The signed line is the claim, not the proof.',
        'r2': 'A warehouse posts a card that each picker signs once a year, saying they know how to report a leaking drum. The signed card goes in a binder. The binder does not show who actually called out when a drill drum appeared on the floor. In the drill, some pickers reported the drum and some walked past it. The manager thanked the people who reported it and left the old signatures as they were. A signed card from a supplier that says every drum is sealed can still sit beside a wet pallet. In the real lesson, the card is the acknowledgement and the drill count is the behavior the signature does not prove. The wet pallet is the contradicting exhibit, which the signature cannot erase.',
        'r4': 'Name the rule, the date, and the signer role on every acknowledgement. Keep the roster of missing names and chase those people. After signatures are in, measure the behavior the rule names. Record the count beside the roster. Do not edit historical signatures so they match a later hope. Do not treat a vendor signature as a finished control. Compare vendor claims with exhibits and reports. Teach again when the count is weak. Look at a confusing step before you blame staff. Leave the old roster as it was while you add the new count.',
        'exam': 'Choose this when a person or a vendor formally states that they understand a rule. The signature, tick, or annual click is the statement, not proof they follow it. Do not choose this for the person the data is about. Do not choose this for a deletion duty that applies when the law requires a record to be removed. Do not choose this for a list of what you hold, where it is, and how long you keep it. A clean roster beside a weak report count is the classic trap.',
    },
    'Data subjects': {
        'body': "Data subjects are the people the records describe. A data subject is the person the data is about, and that person can have rights to know what you hold, to correct it, and sometimes to delete it. The subject is not the file, not the database, and not the team that stores the row. A patient, a staff member, and a customer can each be a subject when a row is about them. Mixing the person up with the system that holds the row is how requests land in the wrong queue.\n\nThe rights run through a named process with an owner. A request to see a copy should reach that owner, not a random pile of tickets. A correction should change the record the organization actually uses, including the copies an inventory says exist, or it should record why a copy cannot be changed. A deletion request, where a right to be forgotten applies, is checked against a legal hold before anyone wipes a mailbox. The inventory says what you have, where it sits, and how long you keep it. Without that inventory, a promise to the subject is a guess about drawers you have not opened. A plain request for one's own record is enough when the speaker is that person.\n\nOn 9 May, Nora asked the clinic for a copy of her own chart. The front desk opened ticket 882 and routed it to the records owner, not to the general support queue. The inventory showed the chart in the clinical system, a billing export on a file share, and a mailbox folder kept by the nurse who coordinated her visits. The copy covered those three places after a misfiled name was removed from the billing export. The procedure required an answer within 30 days, and the copy went out on day 6. A deletion was not requested. If one had been, the owner would have checked the hold list before wiping anything. The ticket line named subject Nora, a copy request, three locations, and no deletion.\n\nWrite the request path where staff can find it, name the owner, and time the response against the limit that applies. Check identity before releasing a chart, so one person's right does not become another person's leak. When a correction is made, look for the other copies the inventory listed. When deletion is barred by a hold or by a duty to keep a medical record, say so and keep the record. Do not drop the request into a generic queue and hope. A staff signature about understanding privacy is not the same as serving this person. The signature is a statement. The subject is the human the row describes. An acknowledgement is someone saying they understand a rule. An inventory is the list of stores and retention periods. A right to be forgotten, where it applies, is the deletion duty, including copies, unless a hold or another duty says to keep the data. The separating fact is the human: this is the person the data is about, and the request belongs to the owner of that process.",
        'r0': "A data subject is the person a record describes, with a process to see a copy, to correct it, and sometimes to delete it. Ticket 882 on 9 May routed Nora's chart request to the records owner, not the general queue. The inventory listed three locations: the clinical system, a billing export, and a nurse mailbox. The procedure required an answer within 30 days, and the copy went out on day 6 after another patient's misfiled name was removed. Identity was checked before release. No deletion was requested. If it had been, a legal hold would have been checked before any mailbox was wiped. The inventory is how copies are found. The subject is the person, not the store and not a staff signature about privacy.",
        'r1': 'This is the person the data is about. A patient can be one. A staff member can be one. A customer can be one. They may ask to see a copy. They may ask to correct the record. They may sometimes ask for deletion. Send the request to the process owner. Do not drop it in a random queue. Check identity before you release a chart. Look for other copies when you correct one. Check a legal hold before any deletion. An inventory tells you where the copies sit. A signed privacy promise is not this person.',
        'r2': 'A building keeps a file for each resident, and the file is about that person, not about the cabinet. The resident may ask for a copy, ask for a wrong phone number to be fixed, and sometimes ask for the file to be destroyed. The request goes to the manager named for records, not into a lost box at the front desk. Before anyone shreds paper, the manager checks whether a court says to keep it. A cabinet list shows how long each drawer is kept. A guard note that says the guard understands privacy is not the resident. In the real case, the resident is the data subject and the copy request is the right to know what is held. The cabinet list is the inventory, which is a different tool from the person.',
        'r4': "Post the request path where the front desk can see it. Name the owner of the process. Check identity before you release a copy. Route the ticket to that owner, not to a random queue. Use the inventory to find other copies when you correct a record. Check a legal hold before any deletion. Do not delete yet if a hold is still open. Do not mix another person's data into the copy. Say so when a duty to keep the record blocks deletion. Time the answer against the procedure limit.",
        'exam': 'Choose this when the question is about the person the data describes. That person may seek a copy, a correction, or sometimes deletion. Do not choose this for a staff or vendor signature that only says a rule is understood. Do not choose this for the inventory of stores and retention periods, which is how you find the copies. Do not choose this for the deletion rule itself when the stem is about whether a hold blocks a wipe. The subject is the human who can make the request.',
    },
    'Right to be forgotten': {
        'body': "Right to be forgotten is a person's request that personal data about them be erased. Where the law gives the request force, the erasure reaches copies you hold and copies a supplier holds for you, unless an exception applies. The request is not a backup retention schedule. A schedule says how long a class of backups or records is kept for everyone in that class. The request names one person and asks for erasure of that person's data. A legal hold, a statute that requires you to keep a record, or a need to defend a claim can stop the erasure. Those exceptions are part of the rule, not a way to ignore the person.\n\nThe work starts with identity and with the hold check, before anyone clears a mailbox or purges a ticket. If a hold covers the mailbox, the wipe waits and the person receives an explanation. If no exception applies and the law requires erasure, active systems are cleared and the suppliers who hold copies are told. Backup tapes are not magically rewritten the same afternoon. The honest record says which active copies were deleted, which suppliers were notified, and which tape will age out under its existing retention clock, with a rule that a restore must not bring the person back into a live system. Pretending the schedule itself answered the person leaves the mailbox in place.\n\nTicket PR-219 arrived from a patient who wanted a marketing mailbox erased. The privacy desk confirmed identity, then searched the hold list before any delete key was used. An active litigation hold covered that mailbox, so the mailbox stayed, and the reply explained the hold in plain language. A seven-year backup rule for clinical images was not used as the answer, because that rule is a retention schedule for a class of images, not a decision about this person's marketing mail. The ticket logged the identity check, the hold result, the systems looked at, and the refusal reason. A second request the next month, for a newsletter address with no hold and no keep-duty, was deleted from the active list and from the mail vendor the same week.\n\nA defender cannot finish this request from memory. The map of where data sits has to exist first, or a forgotten export survives the wipe. Students call any destruction rule this right, including a backup calendar that never names a person. The fact that separates them is who is asking and what is being decided: one person's erasure request, tested against exceptions, versus a schedule that sets how long a whole class of copies is kept.",
        'r0': "Right to be forgotten is a person's erasure request, not a backup retention schedule. Where the law applies, deletion includes supplier-held copies unless an exception such as a legal hold, a statutory keep-duty, or defense of a claim applies. Ticket PR-219 was checked against the hold list before any mailbox delete, and an active litigation hold produced a written refusal rather than a wipe. A seven-year image schedule did not answer that marketing-mail request. When erasure is required, clear active systems, notify suppliers, suppress the person if a backup is later restored, and record tape expiry instead of claiming an instant tape rewrite. The log needs identity result, hold result, systems, suppliers, and any explained refusal.",
        'r1': 'A person can ask that personal data be erased. That ask is the right to be forgotten. A backup retention schedule is different. The schedule times a whole class of copies. The request names one person. Check a legal hold before any wipe. An active hold means the mailbox stays. Tell the person why, in plain words. Include copies a supplier holds when the law says so. Confirm identity before destruction. Erase active systems when no exception applies. Do not claim every tape was rewritten today. Write down what was deleted and what was kept.',
        'r2': "A former shopper asks a clinic desk to destroy the reminder cards kept under that shopper's name. The storeroom still has a calendar that says shred every box of old registers after seven years. The calendar never heard this shopper's name, and following the calendar does not answer the shopper. If a lawyer has already said those cards must be kept for a case, the cards stay and the shopper is told why. Cards in the active drawer can still be pulled when no such order exists, while a sealed crate in the basement waits for its ordinary shred date. At the privacy desk, the shopper's ask is the right to be forgotten and the seven-year calendar is the retention schedule. One names a person, and the other times a class of boxes.",
        'r4': "Confirm identity before any deletion. Search legal holds before clearing a mailbox. Stop the wipe when a hold or a keep-duty applies, and explain why. Erase active copies when the law requires erasure. Notify suppliers who hold copies for you. Do not describe a backup schedule as the person's answer. Suppress the person if a backup is restored. Record tape expiry instead of claiming an instant rewrite. Log systems, dates, and any refusal. Use the data map so a forgotten export is not missed.",
        'exam': "Right to be forgotten is a person's request that personal data be erased. It is not a backup retention schedule, which times a class of copies without naming that person. Where the law applies, include copies, unless a legal hold or another keep-duty applies. Check the hold before a mailbox wipe. An active hold means you do not wipe, and you explain the refusal. Instant tape rewrites are not the requirement. Deleting one row and hoping is the trap when other copies remain.",
    },
    'Data inventory and retention': {
        'body': "A data inventory says what you have, where it sits, and how long you keep it. Retention without that list is a guess wearing a schedule's clothes. The list is not a person's request to be erased. The request, where the law applies, asks for one person's data to be deleted after exceptions are checked. The inventory is the map that makes either keeping or deleting possible. A schedule that says seven years means nothing if nobody knows which folder the seven years apply to. A legal hold pauses destruction for the rows it names, and the pause should be visible on those rows rather than remembered in a meeting.\n\nFields worth keeping are the data type, the owner, the system, the location, the classification, who else receives it, and the event that starts the destruction clock. Triggers sound like last treatment plus seven years, or end of contract plus the backup cycle. Forever, because disk is cheap, fails the first erasure demand and the first storage bill that matters. Supplier tenants belong on the list, because a copy in a vendor system is still your problem to know about. Cardholder rows should say whether a full card number exists or a token stands in its place, and they should say that security codes are not kept after authorization.\n\nThe clinic team found an export of patient names on an open file share that the inventory had never listed. The file was a forgotten extract from a reporting job two years earlier. Under the schedule, that class of extract was due for deletion one year after the report was published, so the extract was removed the same day and the share permission was tightened for the parent folder. The inventory gained a row for reporting extracts, with the job owner and the one-year clock. Nobody called that cleanup a person's erasure request. No individual had asked. The schedule and the newly found location did the work. The deletion job's log was kept so the row was more than a policy sentence.\n\nA defender updates the inventory when a new system, a new export, or a new supplier appears, and checks destruction jobs against the clocks. A person's access, correction, or erasure request is impossible to finish honestly if the map is missing the folder. Students treat the retention schedule as the right to be forgotten, or they treat a policy PDF as the inventory. The fact that separates this topic is the map plus the clock: what exists, where it sits, and when that class of data goes.",
        'r0': "Record data type, owner, system, location, classification, recipients, and the destruction trigger. A schedule with no such list cannot be executed. A forgotten export of patient names on an open share, two years old, was deleted the same day because that class of extract was due one year after publication, and the share was then tightened. Supplier tenants are locations. Cardholder rows state whether full numbers exist, whether tokens replace them, and that security codes are not kept after authorization. A legal hold pauses the clock for the named rows. The inventory makes a person's erasure request possible and does not itself decide that person's legal grounds. Evidence includes destruction-job logs, not only the policy document.",
        'r1': "List what you hold and where it sits. List how long each set is kept. A schedule without that list is a guess. Include supplier systems, not only your own. Add a row when a new export appears. A forgotten file share still counts. Delete or protect that file under the clock. Name an owner for every set. Pause destruction when a legal hold hits a row. Keep security codes off the card rows. Say whether you store a token or a full number. This map is not one person's erasure request. Keep the log that shows a deletion job ran.",
        'r2': "A warehouse paints each shelf with what belongs there, who owns the pallet, and the date the pallet must leave. A calendar in the office that says discard after seven years cannot find a pallet with no label. Someone still has to walk the aisles, because a box behind the door is easy to forget. If a lawyer tags one pallet, that pallet stays past the date, and the tag is visible on the shelf. Throwing out one customer's folder because that customer asked is a different errand from the dated sweep of a whole aisle. In the records room, the shelf labels are the data inventory and the dated sweep is retention. A single customer's ask to destroy a folder is the right to be forgotten, which needs the labels and is not the same sweep.",
        'r4': "Write the data type, owner, system, and location for each set. Add the event that starts the destruction clock. Include supplier copies and forgotten exports. Remove or protect a stray patient-name file under the schedule. Record whether card data is a token or a full number. Confirm security codes are not kept after authorization. Mark legal holds on the rows they freeze. Keep logs of destruction jobs. Update the list when a new system appears. Do not treat the list as a decision on one person's erasure request.",
        'exam': "Data inventory and retention answer what you have, where it sits, and how long that class of data is kept. A schedule without the list is a guess. A forgotten export has to be protected or deleted under the clock. This topic is not the right to be forgotten, which is one person's erasure request. A legal hold pauses destruction for the rows it names. Card rows should show tokens versus full numbers, and security codes should not remain after authorization. A policy PDF with no locations is the trap.",
    },
    'Internal audits': {
        'body': "Internal audits are checks in which your own team compares daily practice with the rules. The team sits inside the organization, looks at how the work is actually done, and writes a finding when practice misses the rule. The check is useful. It is not a substitute for an outside look when a contract, a standard, or a regulator requires one. A finding from your own people still counts. Familiarity is not a reason to soften it.\n\nThe work is a planned comparison of practice to a stated rule, with evidence, a finding, an owner, and a date to look again. The auditor should be able to show the rule, the sample, and the gap. A slide that says the team cares about security is not a finding. Sampling might cover badge reviews, access removal, backup tests, or whether incident notes match the procedure. The report goes to someone who can require a change, not only to the people who were checked. Tracking uses the same fields as any other finding: open, owner, due date, and later proof that the gap closed. A clean opinion from inside the building does not replace an outside party's request for evidence, and it does not replace a regulator visit.\n\nIn March the clinic audit team sampled badge reviews against a written rule that every badge list is reviewed within 30 days. The facilities office was 46 days past due. Finding 14 said the badge review was late, the rule was 30 days, the observation was 46 days, and the owner was facilities. The finding was logged in the same tracker the security office uses for vendor gaps. Nobody marked it informal because the auditors work down the hall. A second check was set for 15 May. The outside report a host provides, and the examiner who might ask for a breach drill, stayed separate events. Finding 14 did not claim to be either of those.\n\nTreat the finding as real work. Write the rule, the sample, and the gap so a later reader can see them without an oral story. Do not edit the finding to protect a colleague. Do not close a finding when someone promises to try. Close a finding when the evidence shows the practice matches the rule, or accept the remaining risk in writing with a named owner. Keep this file even when you also buy an outside review. If a required outside review is missing, the internal file does not fill that hole. Schedule the next sample so the check is a habit, not a rescue after one bad week. Students call every review an audit and then mix up who is in the room. An external audit is an outside party checking your controls or your books against a standard, and that party asks for evidence rather than a slogan. A regulatory examination is a regulator looking at the law, with consequences if you do not meet it. The separating fact is the team: it is your own team, checking practice against the rules, and the finding still has an owner.",
        'r0': "Run the check in four phases: planning, fieldwork, reporting, and follow-up. Planning names the rule. Fieldwork samples practice. Reporting writes the gap. Follow-up checks the fix. In March the rule was a badge review at least every 30 days. The facilities sample was 46 days past due. Finding 14 recorded the rule, the 46-day observation, and the facilities owner, and a second check was set for 15 May. The finding stayed in the same tracker as other gaps. It was not marked informal because the auditors work inside the organization. This file does not replace an outside party's evidence request or a regulator visit later. A slide about caring is not a phase and not a result.",
        'r1': 'Your own team checks practice against the rules. The team sits inside the organization. A miss still counts as a finding. This check is not an outside review. This check is not a regulator visit. Show the rule, the sample, and the gap. A caring slide is not evidence. Give the report to someone who can require a change. Track an owner, a due date, and later proof. Do not soften a finding for a colleague. Do not close a finding on a promise. Close a finding when practice matches the rule. A missing outside review stays missing. Plan the next sample on a calendar.',
        'r2': 'A warehouse crew checks its own aisles against the posted rule that every spill is logged within a day. The crew works for the same company it checks. A late log is still written up, with a name and a date to look again. Friendliness in the break room does not erase the late log. A visitor hired from outside, who asks to see the logs, is a different event. A city officer who can close the building is another event. The crew speech at the morning meeting is not the log. In the real check, the crew is your own team and the late spill log is a finding against the rule. The outside visitor and the city officer stay separate, because this check does not replace them.',
        'r4': 'Write the rule, the sample, and the gap in the finding. Name an owner and a date to check again. Give the report to someone who can require the change. Do not soften the wording for a colleague. Do not close the finding yet. Close the finding only when new evidence shows practice matches the rule, or accept the remaining risk in writing. Keep the internal file even if you also buy an outside review. Do not offer this file as a substitute when an outside review is required. Schedule the next sample.',
        'exam': 'Choose this when your own team checks practice against the rules and writes a finding. The finding still needs an owner. Do not choose this for an outside party reviewing controls or books against a standard. Do not choose this for a regulator who is testing whether you meet the law and can impose a consequence. A slide that says the team cares is not the check. Familiarity is not a reason to drop the gap. This file does not fill in for an outside review the rules require.',
    },
    'External audits': {
        'body': "An external audit is an outside party checking your controls, or your books, against a standard. The useful part is evidence, not a slogan. The outside party is not your own audit team, which can check the organization and still is not a substitute when an outside look is required. The outside party is also not the regulator sitting in the room to decide whether you meet the law, with consequences if you do not. An external audit can be serious and still lack the regulator's power to close an activity. What the auditor should receive is the record that shows a control ran: tickets, samples, dates, and exceptions.\n\nThe request is usually specific. Access reviews, backup tests, joiner and leaver timing, and vendor packets are the sort of artifacts that match a control statement. A slide that says the clinic cares about security answers none of those requests. Scope matters: a review of one region or one product does not automatically describe a region you added later. The period matters: a report about last year does not describe a control you invented last month. Exceptions and customer duties listed in an outside packet still have to be read. Filing the cover and skipping the findings repeats the same mistake people make with a logo.\n\nIn April an external audit firm asked for evidence of access reviews for the first quarter. The request named a sample of twenty-five privileged accounts, the dates the reviews were completed, and the tickets that removed stale rights. The clinic produced the review spreadsheet, the twenty-five account rows, and three removal tickets from February. A slide titled with a security motto was not included, because the firm had not asked for a motto. One sampled account still had access three weeks after the person left, and that row became a finding with an owner and a due date. The finding was tracked the same way an internal finding would be tracked. The regulator was not in the room, and nobody treated the firm's visit as a license hearing.\n\nA defender prepares evidence before the visit, assigns an owner to each request, and writes findings into the same tracking used for any other gap. Coaching staff to hide a stale account is how a difficult review becomes a worse one. Students pick this label for any inspection, including a self-check and a regulator's examination. The fact that separates an external audit is an outside party testing your evidence against a standard, without that party being the regulator who can shut the door.",
        'r0': 'An external audit is an outside party testing controls or books against a standard, and the deliverable they need is evidence. One April request covered first-quarter access reviews: a sample of twenty-five privileged accounts, completion dates, and the tickets that removed stale rights. A motto slide is not that evidence. A stale account still enabled three weeks after departure becomes a finding with an owner and a due date. Scope and period limit what the opinion covers, so a different region or a later month is not included by wish. This visit is not an internal self-check and not a regulatory examination, which is the regulator applying the law with consequences. Exceptions still have to be read.',
        'r1': "An outsider checks your controls against a standard. Give evidence, not a motto slide. Access reviews need dates and tickets. A sample of accounts is normal. A stale account becomes a finding. Give the finding an owner and a date. Scope and time limit the opinion. Another region is not included by hope. Your own team's check is a different visit. A regulator in the room is a different visit. Read the exceptions, not only the cover. Do not hide a bad row. Track the finding like any other gap.",
        'r2': "An outside inspector walks a warehouse and asks for the log of who still holds a key, including the dates those keys were checked. A poster about caring for the goods does not answer the question. The warehouse owner walking the same aisle next month is a useful habit and is not this inspector. A city officer who can padlock the door is a different visit with a different power. If one key still works for a person who left, the inspector writes that down and expects a name beside the repair. In the audit, the inspector is the external auditor and the key log is the access-review evidence. The owner's walk is an internal audit, and the city officer is a regulatory examination.",
        'r4': "Read the request for the exact evidence, period, and scope. Pull access-review dates and removal tickets, not a motto slide. Match the sample size the auditor named. Record stale access as a finding with an owner. Track that finding in the ordinary gap list. Note which region and which months the opinion covers. Read exceptions before you brief leadership. Do not coach anyone to hide a row. Keep this visit separate from a regulator's examination. Keep it separate from your own internal audit as well.",
        'exam': 'An external audit is an outside party checking evidence against a standard. Access-review tickets and a sample of accounts fit this label. A motto slide does not. An internal audit is your own organization checking itself. A regulatory examination is the regulator applying the law, with consequences. Do not swap the three. Scope and period limit the opinion. Hiding a stale account is the trap. Findings still need an owner and a date.',
    },
    'Regulatory examinations': {
        'body': "Regulatory examinations are a regulator's look at whether you meet the law, with consequences if you do not. The person in the room, or on the call, has authority that a hired auditor does not. A missed point can become a penalty, a required change, or a limit on how you operate. This visit is not your own team checking its practice. It is not an outside firm you hired to review controls against a standard you chose.\n\nThe work is a request for evidence against a legal duty. Examiners ask for procedures, records of drills, notices you sent, and proof that people followed the steps. What you produce should be the document you actually use, not a slide written the night before. The breach procedure, the last drill record, access reviews, and complaint logs are typical asks. Counsel usually owns the channel, so staff do not guess which draft is the one in force. If a rule gives a set number of hours to notify, the examiner will look at the clock you actually used. A contract can demand a tighter clock than the law, and that tighter clock is a contract duty, not this exam, unless the law itself requires it. During the visit, produce what was asked, note what you could not find, and do not invent a record. Afterward, findings come with deadlines.\n\nOn 18 September an examiner asked the clinic for the breach procedure and the last drill record. The procedure on file was dated 11 January. The last drill record was dated 18 June and named the people who practiced the notice steps. The team produced both the same afternoon, through counsel. Exam log 18 recorded the 11 January procedure, the 18 June drill, that both were produced, and that no draft was swapped in. A badge review finding from the clinic audit team stayed in a different folder and was not offered as the answer to the examiner. A report from the payroll host was not offered as if the regulator had written it.\n\nKeep the documents an examiner is likely to ask for in a known place, current, and matched to drills you actually ran. If you have notice, walk the request before the day and fix gaps you can fix honestly. Do not change a procedure date. Do not rewrite a drill record to look earlier. If a record is missing, say so and show the gap. Keep this visit separate from the internal finding log and from a vendor outside report. Those files can help you prepare. They are not the examination. When findings come back, assign an owner and a date, and keep the letters. Students treat any person with a checklist as the regulator. Your own team checking practice against the rules is an internal audit. An outside party you hired, or a firm checking controls against a published standard, is an external audit. The separating fact is legal authority: a regulator is examining whether you meet the law, and the consequences are legal rather than a note in your own tracker.",
        'r0': 'A regulator examines evidence against a legal duty and can attach a consequence. On 18 September the examiner asked for the breach procedure dated 11 January and the drill record dated 18 June. Both were produced the same afternoon through counsel. Exam log 18 noted that no draft was substituted and that dates were not altered. A 72-hour notice clock, where the law imposes one, is checked from the real start time rather than from a schedule someone rewrote. The clinic finding on badge reviews was not offered as the answer. The payroll host report was not offered as the regulator work. Missing records are stated as gaps. Later findings get an owner and a deadline. Changing a drill date would falsify the record the examiner asked to see.',
        'r1': 'A regulator checks whether you meet the law. Consequences follow if you do not. This is not your own team check. This is not a firm you hired. Produce the real procedure, not a fresh slide. Produce the drill record they ask for. Counsel should own the channel. Do not guess which draft is in force. Do not invent a missing record. Do not change a document date. Say so when a record is absent. Separate this visit from your own finding log. A vendor report is not the regulator letter. Assign an owner when findings come back.',
        'r2': 'The fire marshal walks in with authority the building staff do not have over themselves. The marshal asks for the evacuation plan and the record of the last drill, not for a poster painted the night before. If a stair is blocked, the consequence can be a fine or a limit on using the hall. A crew that checked its own aisles last month cannot hand that note over as if it were the marshal visit. A consultant the building hired to look at locks is not the marshal either. Staff do not rewrite the drill record to show an earlier date. In the real visit, the marshal is the regulator and the evacuation plan is the procedure you must produce. The consequence is legal, which separates this visit from your own check and from a consultant you paid.',
        'r4': 'Keep the breach procedure and the latest drill record where counsel can reach them. Produce the documents the examiner names. Do not swap in a slide written overnight. Do not change dates on procedures or drill records. Say so when a record is missing, and show the gap. Keep your own audit findings in their folder. Do not present a vendor report as the regulator work. Write the exam log with what was requested and what was produced. Assign an owner and a date to each finding that comes back.',
        'exam': 'Choose this when a regulator examines whether you meet the law and a consequence can follow. Do not choose this for your own team checking practice against the rules. Do not choose this for an outside firm you hired to review controls against a standard. A contract clock that is tighter than the law is a contract duty unless the law itself sets that clock. Do not treat a rewritten date as if the duty were met. A missing record should be stated, not invented. The authority in the room is the separating fact.',
    },
    'Penetration test types': {
        'body': "Penetration tests come in named kinds, and the kind is part of the question the test can answer. A technical test tries systems, applications, and exposed services. A physical test tries doors, badges, and locks. A single engagement can include both when the written scope says both. Every kind here is a permitted attempt to find a weakness and show impact, inside rules that name targets, hours, and a stop. A discussion in a conference room, with nobody sending a message and nobody walking a lobby, is a tabletop. The tabletop can be useful and still is not a penetration test, because no live step tried to get in.\n\nThe report should say which kind was run, not which kind was advertised in a sales line. A phishing note inside a technical scope is still a live message, with permission, aimed at showing whether a person or a mailbox control fails. That live note is not an awareness drill whose only goal is to teach and to praise a report. The amount of inside knowledge the tester receives is a separate choice from the kind of test. Whether the week is scored on break-in success, on detection and response, or on both, is a separate choice again. Mixing those axes produces a report that sounds busy and answers none of the questions cleanly.\n\nA clinic scope for the May engagement named two kinds: a phishing note to a listed set of staff mailboxes, and a lobby visit during business hours. The note was sent on 6 May. The lobby visit happened on 7 May, and the tester stopped at the inner door named in the rules. The report used two headings so a reader could see both results. A tabletop the previous month had talked through a similar story and sent nothing. That tabletop was filed under exercises, not under this report. The tester's packet for the May work included a standard user account and no network diagram, which is a fact about knowledge, recorded in its own paragraph so nobody would pretend the kind of test and the amount of knowledge were the same fact.\n\nA defender reads the scope before the week starts and rejects a report that claims a kind the scope never allowed. Life-safety networks and emergency exits stay out even if a kind of test could reach them. Students label every security conversation a penetration test, or they treat a physical result as if it proved a software flaw. The fact that separates the types is the kind of attempt that was permitted and actually performed.",
        'r0': 'Name the kind in the scope and again in the report: technical, physical, or both. A May clinic scope required a phishing note to a listed mailbox set on 6 May and a lobby visit on 7 May that stopped at the inner door. A prior-month discussion that sent no message was a tabletop and was filed separately. Knowledge given to the tester is a different axis, so a standard user account with no diagram must be stated on its own. Scoring break-in success is different again from scoring detection and response. Rules still forbid life-safety networks and any step that would block an emergency exit. The report headings should match the kinds that were actually run.',
        'r1': 'A penetration test has a named kind. Technical work tries systems and services. Physical work tries doors and badges. The scope can require both. Permission and a stop line come first. A room discussion with no live step is not this test. A live phishing note is not a training-only drill. Say which kind was actually run. How much the tester was told is a separate fact. Whether you score detection is a separate fact. Do not let a lobby result stand in for a software flaw. Keep life-safety gear out. Match the report headings to the scope.',
        'r2': "A fire crew can schedule two different checks in one week because the work order names both: a hose test on the hydrant, and a walk through the lobby doors. Each check is written up under its own heading. A meeting where people talk about a fire and nobody touches a hose is a different kind of afternoon. Knowing the building's master key is a separate choice from which check was ordered. Scoring whether the alarm desk noticed the walk is a separate choice again. In the engagement, the hose test and the door walk are two penetration-test types, and the talk with no hose is a tabletop. The work order is the scope, which has to say which kinds will actually happen.",
        'r4': 'Read the scope for each named kind before the week starts. Require written permission and a stop line. File a discussion-only session as a tabletop, not as this test. Separate a live phishing note from a training-only drill. Record how much inside knowledge the tester received. Record whether detection was being scored. Keep life-safety networks and emergency exits out. Reject report headings the scope never allowed. Do not treat a lobby result as proof of a software flaw. Give each kind its own section in the report.',
        'exam': 'Penetration test types are named kinds of permitted attempts to show impact: technical, physical, or both, as the scope states. A phishing note plus a lobby visit is two kinds, and the report should separate them. A tabletop is a discussion, not this test, and it is not a live phishing exercise. How much the tester knows is a different axis. Whether the week scores detection and response is a different axis. A physical result does not prove a software flaw. A sales label that was not actually run is the trap.',
    },
    'Known, partial, and unknown environments': {
        'body': "How much the tester is told changes the question a test can answer. A known environment means the tester is given internal knowledge: diagrams, configuration, and often a privileged account. That run shows depth, such as what a stolen administrator laptop could reach. An unknown environment means little or no inside knowledge, closer to a stranger who starts from a public name. A partial environment means some knowledge, such as one ordinary account, with the rest withheld. Each result is valid only for the knowledge that was actually provided. A report that hides that fact invites someone to treat a stranger's result as an insider's result.\n\nThe older color nicknames map cleanly if you keep the direction straight. Known lines up with what people call a white-box test, because the inside is visible to the tester. Unknown lines up with a black-box test, because the tester starts in the dark. Partial lines up with a gray-box test, because some lights are on. Students reverse the words. The everyday word known sounds like known to the defender, as if the defender already found the flaw. In this label, known means known to the tester. Unknown means the tester was not handed the inside. Partial is the middle, and the report should list the exact facts that were handed over.\n\nA clinic ran three scoped exercises in one quarter, and the summaries said so in the first paragraph of each. The unknown exercise received only the public website name and had to discover everything else. The known exercise received the network diagram and a domain administrator account, modeling a stolen admin laptop. The partial exercise received one standard user account and no diagram. The unknown summary spoke to what a stranger could reach from the outside. The known summary spoke to how far that administrator account could go once it existed. The partial summary spoke to a normal employee account and refused to claim either of the other answers. None of the three was a tabletop, because each included live steps inside the rules.\n\nA defender writes the knowledge level next to the finding before anyone ranks business risk. A missing patch found with a domain administrator account is not the same urgency story as the same patch found with no credentials, and it is not a reason to pretend the stranger could not have found a different door. The kind of test, physical or technical, is a separate axis. Students pick known whenever the company already knew about a system. The fact that separates the three labels is the amount of internal knowledge the tester was given.",
        'r0': 'A known environment gives the tester internal knowledge, a partial environment gives some, and an unknown environment gives little or no inside knowledge. One clinic quarter kept the three apart: the unknown run received only the public website name, the known run received the network diagram plus a domain administrator account, and the partial run received a single standard user account and no diagram. Known aligns with the white-box nickname, unknown with black-box, and partial with gray-box, and the direction is what the tester was given. A stolen-administrator question belongs to the known run. A stranger question belongs to the unknown run. The report must list the exact aids provided. Knowledge level is not the same fact as physical versus technical, and it is not a tabletop.',
        'r1': 'Known means the tester received internal knowledge. Unknown means little or no inside knowledge. Partial means some knowledge and not the rest. Say exactly what was handed over. A diagram plus an admin account is known. Only a public name is unknown. One normal account and no diagram is partial. Known is not known to the defender. A stranger question needs the unknown result. A stolen-admin question needs the known result. Do not mix the three summaries. Color nicknames must keep that same direction. This choice is not the kind of door you tried. A talk with no live step is neither.',
        'r2': 'A locksmith is sometimes handed every key and the full drawing of a building, sometimes handed only a street address, and sometimes handed a single key to one office. The first visit shows how far a person who already holds the keys can go. The second visit shows what a stranger can learn from the sidewalk. The third visit shows what that one office key is worth, and it does not answer the other two questions. Calling the first visit known to the owner, as if the owner had found every open door, reverses the story. In the test, the full drawing and the keys are a known environment, the street address alone is an unknown environment, and the single office key is partial. The label follows what the tester was given, not what the owner hoped was already fixed.',
        'r4': 'Write the knowledge level in the first paragraph of the summary. List the exact diagrams, accounts, or addresses the tester received. Keep a known result with insider questions such as a stolen admin laptop. Keep an unknown result with stranger questions. Keep a partial result with the limited facts that were shared. Do not reverse known to mean the defender already knew the flaw. Do not merge the three summaries into one ranking. Separate this fact from physical versus technical. Separate it from a discussion-only exercise. Ask which question the scope was written to answer.',
        'exam': 'A known environment means the tester is given internal knowledge. An unknown environment means little or no inside knowledge. Partial means some knowledge. Do not reverse known to mean the defender already knew the flaw. A diagram and a domain administrator account model a stolen admin laptop and belong to known. Only a public name belongs to unknown. One ordinary account and no diagram belongs to partial. White-box, black-box, and gray-box follow that same direction. The kind of test and a tabletop discussion are different axes.',
    },
    'Offensive and defensive testing': {
        'body': 'Offensive testing tries to break in. The tester uses permitted steps to show that a path works and to show the impact. Defensive testing improves detection and response. The defenders are scored on whether they saw the attempt and whether they contained it. An integrated week does both: one side tries, and the other side must notice and act. A tabletop is a discussion. People talk through a story, often a phishing story, and nobody sends a live message. A tabletop is not a live phishing exercise, and it is not either of these tests, because nothing was sent and nothing had to be detected.\n\nThe offensive result names the path, the conditions, and how far the tester got before the stop line. The defensive result names the alert, the time from the action to the alert, and the time from the alert to containment. Isolating a host is containment. Deleting a payload and closing the hole, after evidence is preserved, is a later repair. Scoring only the break-in and ignoring the alert wastes the week if the question was whether the on-call desk works. Scoring only a friendly debrief, with no attempt, answers a planning question and should be labeled as a discussion. Rules of engagement still bind both sides: forbidden zones, a halt word, and a ban on taking real card numbers or clinical files out of the environment.\n\nOn Tuesday a tester sent a payload to a clinic workstation named in the scope. Four minutes later a detection rule wrote an alert with the host name, the user, and the time. The on-call analyst isolated the host at minute twelve. The score sheet recorded the payload result, the four-minute detection, and the isolation time. Isolation was marked as containment, not as the final repair. A tabletop the prior Friday had discussed a similar phishing story for forty minutes and sent nothing. That Friday file stayed in the exercise folder. Nobody described the Tuesday payload as a discussion, and nobody described the Friday talk as a detection score.\n\nA defender briefs the on-call desk before a live payload so the test is not mistaken for an unplanned incident, while still letting the desk show whether the alert and the response work. Hiding the test from the people being measured can be valid when the scope says so, and the scope should say so on purpose. Students call every exercise offensive, or they call a tabletop a live phishing test because the story mentioned a phish. The fact that separates the labels is whether someone tried to break in, whether detection and response were measured, or whether people only talked.',
        'r0': 'Offensive testing tries to break in, and defensive testing improves detection and response. An integrated week scores both. On Tuesday a scoped payload hit a named clinic workstation, a detection rule alerted in four minutes with host, user, and time, and the analyst isolated the host at minute twelve. Isolation is containment. Removing the payload and closing the hole, after evidence is kept, is later repair and was not the score. A Friday tabletop discussed a phishing story for forty minutes, sent no message, and was not a live phishing exercise. Rules still ban taking card numbers or clinical files out, and they still include a halt word. Say in the scope whether the on-call desk is briefed beforehand.',
        'r1': 'Offensive testing tries to break in. Defensive testing checks detection and response. An integrated week does both. Record the path and how far it went. Record the alert and the minutes to see it. Record the minutes until the host was isolated. Isolating the host is containment. A later repair is a different step. A tabletop is only a discussion. A tabletop is not a live phishing exercise. Nobody has to detect a message that was never sent. Brief the rules, including the halt word. Do not take real records out. Say whether the on-call desk was warned.',
        'r2': 'One person tries a marked door in a warehouse while another person watches the alarm desk. The try is successful if the door opens. The watch is successful if the bell sounds and someone walks over and closes the area off. A meeting where staff describe a door and nobody touches it produces neither result. Talking about a fake delivery slip in that meeting does not train the bell, because no slip was ever waved at the real door. Closing the area off is the first response, and fixing the latch comes after notes are written down. In the test week, trying the door is offensive testing and watching the bell is defensive testing. The meeting is a tabletop, not a live attempt and not a detection score.',
        'r4': 'State whether the week scores the break-in, the detection, or both. Name the stop line before any payload. Record alert time, host, and user when a defensive score is in play. Mark host isolation as containment. Keep later repair off that containment line until evidence is preserved. File a discussion that sent nothing as a tabletop. Do not call that discussion a live phishing exercise. Decide in the scope whether the on-call desk is briefed. Ban removal of card numbers and clinical files. Write both scores if the week was integrated.',
        'exam': 'Offensive testing tries to break in. Defensive testing improves detection and response, including whether an alert fired and whether the host was isolated. Isolation is containment, not the final repair. An integrated test does both in one window. A tabletop is a discussion, not a live phishing exercise, because no message is sent and nothing must be detected. Do not call a tabletop either kind of test. Do not describe the break-in attempt as something that keeps systems healthy. The scope decides whether the on-call desk is told in advance.',
    },
    'Physical penetration tests': {
        'body': 'A physical penetration test tries the building. Doors, badges, tailgating, and locks are the material, and the work happens only with permission and with safety rules. The goal is to show whether a person can pass a boundary that should have stopped them, and to stop at the line the rules named. It is not a port scan of a website. It is not a conference-room discussion. A tabletop can talk about the lobby for an hour and never touch a door. The physical test touches the door, or tries to, and then writes down what happened.\n\nSafety is part of the definition, not a footnote. The tester does not use force that could injure a person. The tester does not block an emergency exit. The tester carries a letter or a number the guard can check, and a halt word ends the attempt immediately. Hours are chosen so a clinic is not surprised during a patient emergency unless that timing was the point and everyone agreed. Evidence is a photo, a time, and a description of the boundary that failed or held. Taking documents, badges, or clinical files away is out of bounds even if a desk was unattended. The report describes reach. It does not collect trophies.\n\nAt 08:12 a tester followed a staff member through the clinic lobby door without using a badge. A photo showed the door had not closed fully behind the staff member. The tester stopped at the inner door named in the rules and did not enter the clinical hallway. The on-call facilities number on page one was used to tell the site the attempt had reached the inner door and stopped. No emergency exit was blocked. No one was touched. The finding was tailgating at the lobby door during the morning arrival, with the photo time as evidence. A software scan that same week was written in a different section because it was a different kind of test.\n\nA defender fixes the boundary that failed: door closure, a guard at the peak hour, or a badge check that is real rather than polite. The fix is a building control. That repair does not show that any software was tested. Students call any visit to the office a physical test, including a vendor walkthrough that never tried to get past a control. The fact that separates this test is a permitted, safe attempt on doors, badges, tailgating, or locks, ending at the stop line the rules named.',
        'r0': 'A physical test targets doors, badges, tailgating, and locks, with permission, a halt word, and a geographic stop. At 08:12 a tester tailgated through a clinic lobby door, photographed the door that had not closed, and stopped at the inner door named in the rules. No emergency exit was blocked and no force was used. The facilities number on page one was called when the stop line was reached. Evidence is the time, the photo, and the boundary that failed. Removing badges or clinical files is out of bounds. A same-week software scan belongs in a technical section. A discussion that never touched a door is a tabletop, not this test.',
        'r1': 'A physical test tries the building with permission. Doors, badges, tailgating, and locks are the targets. Safety rules are part of the test. Do not injure anyone. Do not block an emergency exit. Carry a way for the guard to check you. Stop at the door the rules named. A photo and a time are the evidence. Do not take files or badges away. A website scan is a different test. A meeting about the lobby is not this test. Fix the door or the badge habit that failed. Write the stop line into the report.',
        'r2': 'A warehouse allows a planned check of the loading dock. The visitor follows a worker through the dock door, notes that the door stayed open, and stops at a yellow line on the floor. Nobody is shoved, and the fire exit stays clear. The visitor has a phone number the dock lead can call to confirm the visit is allowed. A conference room chat about the dock, with everyone seated, never tests the door. A scan of the office computers that afternoon is a different job in a different aisle. On the real site, the dock check is a physical penetration test and the yellow line is the stop in the rules. The seated chat is a tabletop, and the computer scan is a technical test.',
        'r4': 'Confirm written permission before anyone approaches a door. Name the stop line, such as an inner lobby door. Forbid force that could injure a person. Forbid blocking any emergency exit. Put the on-call number on the first page. Record the time and a photo if a door fails. Stop when the named line is reached and call it in. Do not remove badges, files, or clinical papers. File software results in a separate section. Describe the building fix, such as door closure or a real badge check.',
        'exam': 'A physical penetration test is a permitted, safe attempt on doors, badges, tailgating, or locks. The tester stops at the line the rules name. Tailgating at 08:12 and stopping at the inner door fits this label. A tabletop that only discusses the lobby does not. A technical scan does not. Blocking an emergency exit or using injurious force is outside the test. Taking files or badges away is outside the test. The building fix is the defense, and it does not prove a software flaw was tested.',
    },
    'Phishing campaigns': {
        'body': "Phishing campaigns here are measured exercises that send lookalike messages and count who reports them. They are not criminal attacks. Nobody is trying to steal pay, passwords, or patient files. The point is to see whether people notice and use the report path, and to thank the people who do. Shaming the people who click teaches the wrong lesson and hides the next report.\n\nThe exercise is a planned send with permission from leadership, a harmless message, and a count. The message resembles something staff already receive, such as a payroll note or a package alert, and that resemblance is what makes it a lookalike. The link does not ask for a password and does not install anything. It only records that the link was opened, so a click can be separated from a report. The report button, or the mailbox where suspicious mail is sent, is the action you want. After the window closes, write down how many messages went out, how many people reported them, and how many clicked. Do not publish a list of names for ridicule. You can teach the small group that clicked, in private, what the clue was.\n\nIn May the clinic ran the exercise against 400 staff mailboxes. The lookalike was a payroll note about a missed deposit. Of that group, 146 people reported it with the button and 19 clicked. No password was collected. The awareness log carried a line for the May exercise, 400 lookalike payroll notes, 146 reports, 19 clicks, reporters thanked, and a note that this was not a criminal attack. The reporters were thanked the same week. The people who clicked were offered a short coaching note. Names were not posted in the break room. The count is kept so a later month can be compared with May and the team can see whether reporting rises.\n\nSet the measure before the send. Reports are the success. Clicks are the signal for more teaching. A click is not a reason to fire someone. Keep the message harmless. Do not attach a harmful file, and do not collect secrets. Tell leadership the numbers. If the report button is hard to find, fix the button rather than blaming the staff. Separate this exercise from a real suspicious message that arrives from outside. When a real one arrives, the person reports it and does not investigate alone. Also separate it from a person or a system acting unlike their usual pattern. That odd behavior is a different topic, and a peer should ask rather than accuse. The exam trap treats the exercise as a crime or as the whole of someone's skill. A criminal phishing message is an attack, and this campaign is not that attack. Recognizing a bad message and using the report path is what a person does with any message, practice or real. Behavior unlike a baseline is a third idea. The separating fact is the measured exercise: lookalike messages go out on purpose, with permission, and the team counts who reports them.",
        'r0': "Run a permitted exercise that sends lookalike messages and counts reports. It is not a criminal attack. In May, 400 staff mailboxes received a payroll lookalike about a missed deposit. The link recorded an open and did not ask for a password or install software. The awareness log showed 146 reports and 19 clicks. Reporters were thanked the same week. The 19 who clicked were coached in private. Names were not posted. The report button is the success count. A button that is hard to find is fixed before the next send. Compare the report count from one month to the next. A real outside lure is handled as a report, not as part of this exercise. Odd behavior that does not match a person's baseline is a separate signal.",
        'r1': 'This is a measured exercise. The team sends lookalike messages. The team counts who reports them. It is not a criminal attack. No one is trying to steal passwords. Thank the people who report. Do not shame the people who click. The link must not ask for a password. The link must not install anything. Write down sends, reports, and clicks. Do not post names for ridicule. A real outside message is a different event. Reporting is the success you wanted. A click is a cue for more teaching.',
        'r2': 'A school schedules a drill, sounds a bell that sounds like the real alarm, and counts who walks to the door and who tells a teacher. The bell is not a real fire, and nobody is trying to burn the building. Children who tell a teacher are thanked. Children who keep working are coached in private, not listed on a wall for laughter. The drill does not ask anyone to hand over a house key. A real smoke smell from the street is a different event, and a child who suddenly runs at midnight is a different worry. In the real exercise, the bell is the lookalike message and the count of children who tell a teacher is the report count. The drill is measured practice, which is not a criminal attack.',
        'r4': 'Get leadership permission before the send. Use a harmless lookalike that staff could really receive. Point the link at a page that records a click and does not ask for a password. Do not collect secrets. Do not attach a harmful file. Count sends, reports, and clicks. Thank the people who report. Coach people who clicked, in private. Do not post names. Do not turn the exercise into a shame list. Fix a report button that is hard to find before you judge the staff. Compare this month report count with last month.',
        'exam': 'Choose this when a measured exercise sends lookalike messages and counts who reports them. It is not a criminal attack, and it is not a trick used to shame staff. Do not choose this for a real lure that arrived from outside and should simply be reported. Do not choose this for the general skill of spotting odd mail, which applies to any message. Do not choose this for behavior that departs from a baseline. Thanking reporters and coaching people who clicked, in private, agrees with the exercise.',
    },
    'Recognizing and reporting suspicious messages': {
        'body': "The useful habit is to spot an odd message and to use the report path. The person does not click, does not open the attachment, and does not investigate alone. Curiosity feels helpful and still spreads the file. A payroll lookalike, a package notice, or a password-reset page can all be the odd item. The report button, or the desk number printed for this purpose, is the action. Forwarding the attachment around the office so colleagues can look is the opposite of reporting. The security queue needs the original message once, from the report path, so headers stay intact.\n\nA planned practice message and a real criminal message can look alike on purpose. The person's job does not change between them. Spot it, report it, and leave the poking to the people who have a safe way to poke. The campaign that sends a practice message is a different activity, run by the organization, with a goal of teaching rather than shaming. A colleague who suddenly behaves unlike their own baseline is a different concern again, because that concern starts from a person's actions rather than from a message that arrived. A meeting that only talks about a fake message is a discussion. Nothing was reported from a live inbox in that meeting.\n\nOn a Thursday morning a payroll clerk saw a message about a bonus spreadsheet from an address one letter off the real payroll name. The clerk used the report button, did not open the sheet, and did not forward it to the rest of the desk. Ticket PHISH-88 reached the queue with the clerk's name, the time, and the intact message. The queue pulled the message into a safe review. The clerk went back to the payroll run. Later the team thanked the report in the staff note. Nobody posted the clerk's name as a cautionary tale. A second person who had forwarded a similar sheet the week before was coached on the button, not mocked in the hallway.\n\nA defender makes the report path obvious, watches the queue, and treats a report as a success even when the message turns out harmless. Shaming a click teaches people to hide the next one. Asking staff to inspect headers and chase the sender turns the clerk into an untrained investigator. Students call the person's report the same thing as the campaign that sent a practice note, or the same thing as a coworker emailing files at midnight. The fact that separates this habit is the spot, the report, and the decision not to poke the message.",
        'r0': "Recognizing and reporting means the recipient spots the oddity and uses the report path without clicking, without opening the attachment, and without investigating alone. Ticket PHISH-88 recorded a payroll clerk who used the report button on a bonus-spreadsheet lookalike, left the message intact, and did not forward it to the desk. The queue, not the clerk, performed the safe review. A practice campaign that sends the note is a different activity from the person's report. A colleague's unusual outbound behavior is a different concern from an inbound message. A discussion that never touches a live inbox does not exercise this habit. Thank the report, and coach a forwarder without a public scolding.",
        'r1': 'Spot the odd message. Use the report button or the published desk number. Do not click. Do not open the attachment. Do not forward it around the office. Do not investigate alone. Leave the safe review to the queue. A practice note and a real note can look alike. Your job stays the same either way. Thank the person who reports. Coach a mistake without public shame. A campaign that sends practice notes is different. A coworker acting oddly is different. A meeting about a fake note is not a report.',
        'r2': "A receiving clerk sees a delivery slip with a logo that is almost right and a request to open the crate immediately. The clerk calls the desk number posted by the door and does not pry the crate. Passing the crate around the room so everyone can guess is how the mess spreads. The company that sometimes sends a fake slip as a drill is doing a different job from the clerk who calls. A coworker who starts carrying unmarked crates out at night is a different worry from the slip itself. In the inbox, the clerk's call is recognizing and reporting the message, and the crate is the attachment. The drill that sends fake slips is the campaign, which is not the same act as the clerk's report.",
        'r4': 'Put a report button or a desk number where people can see it. Tell staff not to click, open, or forward the odd message. Tell them not to investigate alone. Thank reports in a staff note without naming a person as a joke. Coach someone who forwarded a file, in private. Let the queue do the safe review. Keep practice campaigns labeled as campaigns. Do not treat a midnight file transfer as this same habit. Preserve the reported message intact. Track ticket times so the queue can see the delay.',
        'exam': 'Recognizing and reporting means the person spots the odd message, uses the report path, and does not click, forward, or investigate alone. The report button on a payroll lookalike is this habit. The campaign that sent a practice note is a different activity. A colleague who behaves unlike a baseline is a different concern. A discussion about a fake message is not a live report. Shaming a click teaches hiding. Asking the clerk to chase headers is the trap.',
    },
    'Anomalous behavior': {
        'body': "Anomalous behavior is a person or a system acting unlike their own baseline. The cause might be risky, unexpected, or simply a mistake. The first professional move is to ask and to preserve the record, not to accuse. A quiet accountant who has never sent large files after hours, and who suddenly does, has left the baseline. The mail log can show the account, the size, and the time. Those three facts support a question. They do not, by themselves, prove theft. A peer who asks what changed will learn more than a peer who announces a crime in the hallway.\n\nBaselines are local. The night-shift analyst who always works at midnight is not anomalous for working at midnight. The day-shift accountant is. A system can be the actor too: a workstation that starts talking to a country the clinic never uses, or a badge that enters a door the badge owner does not work near. The comparison is against that account's history, not against a vague sense that something feels wrong. Write down the normal pattern before you write down the surprise, or the surprise has nothing to differ from. An inbound message that looks like a payroll fake is a different problem. That problem arrives from outside. This problem is an action by someone, or something, that already has a history with you.\n\nOn a Wednesday the mail log showed the accountant account sending about four hundred megabytes of spreadsheet files at 00:40. The account's prior ninety days had no sends after 18:00 and nothing over twenty megabytes. A peer on the Thursday day shift asked the accountant what the midnight job had been, and also told the security desk. The accountant had been trying to finish a board packet from home and had used a personal timing mistake, not a theft. The files were board drafts, confirmed by the finance lead. The log excerpt stayed in the ticket anyway: account, size, time, and the baseline note. The question prevented both a false accusation and a shrug that would have missed a real theft on a later night.\n\nA defender keeps simple baselines for sensitive accounts, routes the oddity to a person who can ask, and separates mistake from misuse with the answer and the file list. Disabling the account in a panic, before the question, can be the wrong first move when the person is in the middle of legitimate work. If the answer and the files do not fit, then containment, such as cutting the session, belongs to the response playbook. Students treat every oddity as an inbound phish, or they treat every oddity as guilt. The fact that separates this topic is a departure from a baseline, followed by a question rather than a verdict.",
        'r0': "Anomalous behavior is a departure from a person or system's own baseline, and the cause may be risk, surprise, or a mistake. A mail log showed an accountant account sending about four hundred megabytes at 00:40, against a ninety-day baseline of no sends after 18:00 and none over twenty megabytes. The peer asked before accusing, and the security desk still received the log: account, size, time, and baseline. The files were board drafts, so the night was a mistake, and the ticket kept the excerpt anyway. An inbound lookalike message is a different case because it arrives from outside. Cutting a session is a later containment step if the answer and the files do not fit, not the opening accusation.",
        'r1': "Someone or something stopped matching their baseline. The cause may be a mistake. Ask before you accuse. Keep the log while you ask. Note the account, the size, and the time. Compare with that account's own history. A night worker at night may be normal. A day worker at midnight may not. An odd inbound message is a different problem. Do not announce guilt in the hallway. If the files fit a real job, say so. If they do not fit, follow the response steps. Save the excerpt either way.",
        'r2': "A quiet cook who has always left by evening is seen carrying large boxes out at midnight. A coworker asks what the boxes are, and also tells the manager, instead of shouting theft across the dining room. The cook was moving a late catering order. The question still mattered, because the next midnight boxes might not be a catering order. The kitchen's ordinary closing time is the baseline, and the cook's own history is the comparison, not a rumor about cooks. A fake invoice that arrived in the morning mail is a different event from the cook's own walk out the door. In the office, the midnight boxes are anomalous behavior and the question is the right first response. The fake invoice is an inbound message, which calls for a report rather than a question about the cook's character.",
        'r4': "Write the baseline before you judge the surprise. Capture the account, the size, the time, and the system. Ask the person what changed, without a public accusation. Tell the security desk and keep the log excerpt. Compare the files with a legitimate job the owner can name. Do not treat an inbound lookalike as this same case. Do not disable an account as a reflex. Move to containment if the answer and the files do not fit. Save the ticket even when the cause was a mistake. Refresh baselines when someone's real hours change.",
        'exam': 'Anomalous behavior means a person or a system left their own baseline. The cause may be risk, surprise, or a mistake, so the first move is to ask and to keep the log. A quiet accountant sending large files at midnight fits this label. An inbound suspicious message does not, because that message arrived from outside. A public accusation is the trap. Cutting off the account is not the opening step when the work may be legitimate. If the files do not fit the answer, containment comes from the response playbook.',
    },
    'User guidance and training': {
        'body': 'Guidance and training teach the few behaviors people actually need. How to report an odd message, how to handle data, and when to stop and ask are enough to start. A long catalog of threats does not stick, and a binder that nobody opens is not training. Practice belongs in the first week, while the person is still forming habits, and the practice uses the same button and the same desk the person will use later. A single conversation can introduce the behaviors. Repeating them on a calendar and measuring whether people do them is a neighboring program. The content of what to teach sits here. The cadence and the measurement sit next door.\n\nThe behaviors should be short enough to rehearse. New hires sit with the report button and submit a harmless sample so the queue is not a mystery. They hear the rule that a found USB stick goes to security and is not plugged into a work computer. They hear when to stop: a caller who demands a password, a request to move money, a chart that does not belong on a home printer. Social-engineering pressure, such as fake urgency from someone claiming authority, is a neighboring lesson with its own counter, a callback on a number already trusted. Password uniqueness and the found-drive rule are neighboring habits with their own detail. This topic is the decision to teach a short list and to practice it, not the full text of every habit.\n\nIn the first week of May, six new clinic hires spent twenty minutes on the report button. Each person submitted a harmless sample, and each person saw the ticket number come back. The same session walked a found USB stick to the security drawer and said the stick stays there. Nobody was asked to watch a ninety-minute video first. The facilitator wrote down who attended and which two behaviors were practiced. A policy PDF stayed linked for people who wanted the longer rule, and the PDF was not counted as the training. When one hire later forwarded a real odd attachment, the coach pointed back to the button the hire had already pressed in May, which is a fairer conversation than pointing at a video the hire slept through.\n\nA defender picks the behaviors that match incidents you actually see, practices them with the real tool, and refuses to call an unread document a lesson. Shame does not belong in the session. Students think any annual video satisfies this topic, or they think a password rule and a training session are the same sentence. The fact that separates guidance and training is a short list of behaviors that people practiced, not a policy that only exists on paper.',
        'r0': 'Teach a short list: how to report, how to handle data, and when to stop and ask. In the first week of May, six new hires each spent part of a twenty-minute session submitting a harmless sample through the real report button and walking a found USB stick to the security drawer. Attendance and the two behaviors practiced were written down. A policy PDF was linked and was not counted as the lesson. A ninety-minute video was not a prerequisite. Callback on a known number for a pushy caller is a neighboring lesson. Unique secrets and the ban on mounting found drives have their own detail. Repeating the lesson monthly and measuring reports is the recurring program, not this first practice.',
        'r1': 'Teach a few behaviors people can rehearse. Show how to report an odd message. Show where a found stick goes. Show when to stop and ask. Use the real button, not a slide of the button. Do this in the first week. Write down who practiced. A binder nobody opens is not training. A long video is not required first. A pushy caller is a neighboring lesson. Password detail is a neighboring lesson. Monthly repeats are a neighboring program. Skip shame in the session. Point back to the practice if someone later slips.',
        'r2': 'A kitchen puts new cooks through a short drill on day one: where the fire towel hangs, which sink is for hands, and when to call the lead instead of guessing. The cooks touch the towel and the sink. A poster in the break room is available and is not the drill. A monthly refresher later in the year is a different program from this first morning. A rule about not sharing the freezer code is a specific habit that can be named inside the drill without becoming the whole lesson. In the clinic, the day-one drill is user guidance and training, and the towel is the report button. The monthly refresher is the recurring program, which assumes this first practice already happened.',
        'r4': 'Pick the few behaviors your real incidents need. Practice the report button with a harmless sample in the first week. Practice handing a found USB stick to security. Tell people when to stop and ask. Record who attended and which behaviors they practiced. Do not count an unread policy as the lesson. Leave caller tricks and password detail as neighboring lessons. Leave the monthly measurement to the recurring program. Skip public shame. Point a later mistake back to the practice the person already did.',
        'exam': 'User guidance and training is the short list of behaviors people practice, such as the report button and the found-USB rule in the first week. An unread policy or a video nobody can use is not this topic. A recurring program that repeats lessons and measures them is the neighbor, not the same sentence. Social-engineering callbacks and password rules are neighboring lessons with their own detail. Shame does not belong in the session. The trap is counting a document as practice.',
    },
    'Password and removable-media habits': {
        'body': "Password habits mean unique, long secrets and no sharing. Removable-media habits mean a found drive is not plugged into a work computer. The two habits travel together because both are things a person does with their hands at a desk, and both fail in ordinary, non-dramatic ways. A sticky note on a shared front-desk account is a password failure. A USB stick from the lobby plugged in so someone can see what is on it is a media failure. Social-engineering pressure, such as a caller who uses urgency and a famous name, is a different lesson. The counter to that caller is a callback on a number you already trust. The counter here is a personal secret and a refusal to mount a found drive.\n\nUnique means the clinic password is not the same secret the person uses on a shopping site. Long means guessing and casual shoulder reading get harder. No sharing means the front desk does not pass one password between shifts. A password manager or a sign-in prompt for each person beats a laminated card. When someone leaves, that person's secret can be retired without resetting the whole desk. Found media goes to security, who have a way to look if a look is even appropriate. The desk computer is not that way. A personal phone charger is not the same object as a storage stick, and the habit should say which objects are forbidden so people are not scolded for a charger.\n\nThe front desk had been signing into one shared account with a password written on a sticky note under the keyboard. On 12 June the shared account was disabled, and each clerk received a personal account before the next shift. The sticky note was destroyed. The same week a visitor's USB stick turned up in the lobby planter. The receptionist walked it to security, and security logged it as not mounted on any clinic computer. Nobody inserted it to identify the owner. A caller that afternoon who claimed to be the chief executive and demanded the new password was not given it. The desk used the callback number in the directory. That call was the neighboring lesson, and the ticket said so, separate from the account change.\n\nA defender removes shared secrets, watches for sticky notes on walk-throughs, and gives people an easy place to surrender found drives. A policy sentence that nobody can follow during a busy check-in will be ignored by lunch. Students treat every social trick as a password problem, or they treat a found stick as safe once someone trustworthy is curious. The fact that separates these habits is the secret and the drive: do not share the secret, and do not mount the drive.",
        'r0': 'Password habits require unique long secrets and no sharing. Removable-media habits require that found drives stay out of work computers. On 12 June a shared front-desk account was disabled, personal accounts were issued before the next shift, and the sticky note under the keyboard was destroyed. A visitor USB stick found the same week was logged by security and was not mounted. A caller demanding the new password, even while claiming to be the chief executive, was handled by a callback on a directory number, which is a different lesson. A charger is not a storage stick, and the rule should say so. Walk-throughs look for new sticky notes. A sentence nobody can follow at a busy desk will fail by lunch.',
        'r1': 'Use your own long secret. Do not share it across shifts. Do not reuse the clinic secret on a shopping site. A sticky note under a keyboard is a failure. Give each person an account. Disable the old shared account. A found stick goes to security. Do not plug that stick into a work computer. A phone charger is not a storage stick. A pushy caller is a different lesson. Use the directory number for the return call. Make the safe action easy at a busy desk. Look for new notes on walk-throughs. Write down that a found stick was not mounted.',
        'r2': 'A locker room stops using one combination whispered to every new attendant and instead gives each attendant a personal code. The old combination card comes off the wall the same day. A USB-shaped gadget found on a bench goes to the lost-and-found lead and is not plugged into the front computer to see whose photos are on it. A stranger at the gate who urges everyone to hurry and to shout out a code is a different problem, solved by calling a number the staff already trust. In the clinic, the personal code is the password habit and the bench gadget is the removable-media habit. The hurried stranger is a social-engineering problem, which a new code alone does not solve.',
        'r4': 'Replace shared desk passwords with personal accounts. Disable the shared account the same day. Destroy sticky notes that held the old secret. Require unique secrets that are not reused on outside sites. Tell staff to hand found drives to security. Record that the drive was not mounted. Say that a charger is not a storage stick. Handle a caller who demands a password as a separate callback case. Check desks for new shared notes. Do not write a rule the check-in line cannot follow.',
        'exam': 'Password habits are unique long secrets and no sharing. Removable-media habits mean a found drive is not plugged into a work computer. Disabling a shared front-desk account and issuing personal accounts fits the password half. A USB stick logged and not mounted fits the media half. A caller who uses urgency is a social-engineering case, answered by a callback on a known number. Do not treat that call as solved by the password change alone. Curiosity about a found stick is the trap.',
    },
    'Social-engineering awareness': {
        'body': "Social-engineering awareness is teaching people the tricks. The lesson says that urgency, a claim of authority, and a friendly voice are tools a stranger can use. The counter taught beside those tricks is to slow down and call back on a number you already trust. This class is not a measured send of lookalike mail. It is not a rule about password length or about drives someone found in the parking lot. The teaching gives people a way to refuse a plausible story without feeling rude.\n\nPractice the tricks rather than posting a sign that only says to be careful. Urgency sounds like a deadline in the next ten minutes. Authority sounds like the director, a nurse lead, or a vendor who claims the contract will fail tonight. Friendliness sounds like a helpful caller who already knows a first name and a project name. The counter is the same in each case. Do not use the number the caller gives you. Open the directory, the ticket system, or the contract contact list, and call back on that known number. Ask for a ticket. If the story is real, it survives the callback. If it was a trick, the callback reaches the real person, who did not make the request.\n\nOn 7 September a caller told the clinic desk that the director needed a password reset in the next ten minutes and would be angry if the desk waited. The caller was warm, used the director's first name, and offered a mobile number to use while driving. The desk refused the reset, opened ticket 1904, and called the director on the mobile listed in the staff directory. The director had not called. The ticket recorded urgency, a claim of authority, a caller who pretended to be the director, a callback on the directory number, and a request that was false. The desk was not blamed for the delay. That protection is part of the teaching. People will not slow down if speed is praised and caution is punished.\n\nTeach the tricks with short examples people recognize, then practice the callback until it feels ordinary. New staff should hear it in the first week, and the lesson should return later rather than vanish after one video. Do not reduce the class to a ban on being polite. Politeness stays. The change is which number gets dialed. Rules about long unique secrets, and about not plugging a found drive into a work computer, are different habits. General guidance about the report button still matters, and it is not this lesson. Students fold every class into one label. A measured campaign sends lookalike messages and counts who reports them. That campaign counts mail. It is not the lesson about voice and hurry. The separating fact is the teaching: people learn that urgency, authority, and friendliness are tricks, and that a callback on a known number is the counter.",
        'r0': 'Teach three tricks and one counter. Urgency compresses time, as in a demand for a password reset within ten minutes. Authority borrows a title, such as the clinic director. Friendliness uses a first name and a helpful tone. The counter is a callback on a directory number, not the number the caller offers. On 7 September the desk refused the reset, opened ticket 1904, and reached the director on the listed mobile. The director had not called. Staff who slow down are backed, or the next person will rush. This class is not the measured count of lookalike mail. It is not a rule about unique secrets or found drives. The behavior to practice is the callback and the ticket, repeated until it feels ordinary.',
        'r1': 'This teaching covers the tricks. Urgency is one trick. A claim of authority is another. A friendly voice is another. Slow down before you act. Call back on a number you already trust. Do not use the number the caller gives you. Ask for a ticket. A real request survives the callback. Protect staff who refuse to rush. This is not a count of lookalike mail. This is not a password rule. This is not a rule about found drives. Politeness can stay while the number changes.',
        'r2': "A stranger in a nice coat tells the lobby desk that the building owner needs the spare key in the next ten minutes and will be angry if the desk waits. The stranger is warm, uses the owner's first name, and offers a phone number written on a card. The desk does not hand over the key. The desk opens the house list and calls the owner on the number already printed there. The owner never asked. The desk is praised for the delay. A fire bell drill and a padlock rule are different lessons. In the real teaching, the stranger's hurry, rank, and warmth are the tricks, and the house list call is the callback on a known number. The lesson is how to refuse the trick, which is not the same as counting drill mail.",
        'r4': 'Teach urgency, claimed authority, and a friendly voice as tricks. Practice the callback on a directory number, not on a number the caller dictates. Require a ticket before a reset or a payment change. Tell staff to slow down. Praise a delay that stops a false request. Do not punish the person who refuses to rush. Do not reset the password yet. Do not change the directory number during the call. Repeat the lesson in the first week. Keep this class apart from lookalike counts and from rules about secrets or found drives.',
        'exam': 'Choose this when the lesson teaches the tricks of urgency, a claim of authority, and friendliness. The counter in that lesson is a callback on a number you already trust. Do not choose this for a measured send of lookalike messages that counts reports. Do not choose this for password habits or found drives. Do not choose this for a general class about the report button alone. A caller who borrows the director voice is the stem. Refusing the rush, opening a ticket, and calling the directory number are the matching actions.',
    },
    'Operational security': {
        'body': 'Operational security means not leaking useful details. System names, maintenance schedules, badge habits, and the brand of a door controller are useful to someone who wants to plan. The leak is often ordinary: a job post, a lobby conversation, a photo of a whiteboard, a conference badge line that lists internal host names. The harm is a map handed out for free. This topic is about what you reveal. Where you sit while you work, including a coffee shop and a home printer, is a neighboring set of habits. A monthly training calendar is a neighboring program. The detail in the job post is the problem here.\n\nPeople leak because the detail sounds like competence. A posting that names the exact door-controller model and the remote-access hostname looks precise to a hiring manager and looks like a target list to everyone else. A lobby story about the Saturday maintenance window tells a stranger when alarms and staffing will be thin. A badge left visible in a rideshare photo shows the layout of the credential. The repair is subtraction. Describe the duty, the skills, and the city. Drop the model string, the hostname, and the weekend window. Reviewers who do not run the system should see the public text before it is posted, because the author of the text is the person least likely to notice what it reveals.\n\nA clinic job post for a facilities technician had listed the door-controller model and the hostname vpn.clinic.example. The revised post, approved on 4 August, described badge-system support and remote access as skills and removed both strings. The old post was taken down rather than left archived on a public board. The hiring manager also stopped mentioning the Saturday patch window during lobby tours. A photo of the network whiteboard was cropped before it went into an internal farewell slide that might be mailed onward. None of that work changed the home-printing rule or the coffee-shop screen rule. Those rules still mattered, and they were tracked on a different list because they govern the device in a place, not the secrets in a sentence.\n\nA defender reviews public posts, lobby scripts, and slides the way they review an open share. The question is what a stranger learns. Students call every risky habit this topic, including an unlocked laptop in a cafe. The fact that separates operational security is disclosure of useful detail, in a post or a conversation, rather than the handling of a device away from the building.',
        'r0': 'Operational security withholds useful detail: system names, schedules, badge layout, and equipment models, especially in public posts and lobby talk. A facilities job post that named the door-controller model and the hostname vpn.clinic.example was revised on 4 August to skills-only language, and the old text was taken down. Lobby tours stopped announcing the Saturday maintenance window. A whiteboard photo was cropped before it could travel in mail. Home printing and an unlocked cafe laptop are neighboring habits about the device and the place, not about this disclosure. A monthly awareness calendar is a neighboring program. The test of a sentence is what a stranger can learn from it.',
        'r1': 'Do not publish the internal map. Drop system names from public posts. Drop maintenance windows from lobby talk. Drop equipment model numbers from job ads. Describe the skill and the city instead. Take the old post down, not only the new one. Crop whiteboard photos before they travel. A stranger is the reader to imagine. An unlocked laptop in a cafe is a different habit. Printing records at home is a different habit. A monthly class is a different program. Ask what the sentence reveals. Let someone who does not run the system read the draft.',
        'r2': "A shop's hiring flyer should not list the safe's brand and the night when the safe is open for counting. Those two facts help a stranger more than they help a good applicant. The flyer can still say the job includes closing duties in a named neighborhood. A conversation in the doorway about Saturday counting belongs in the same pile as the flyer. Leaving the cash drawer open on a cafe table is a real problem and is not this flyer. A monthly staff huddle about manners is not this flyer either. In the clinic, the flyer is the job post and the safe's brand is the door-controller model. Operational security is the choice to leave those details out, while device habits away from the building stay a separate list.",
        'r4': 'Read job posts for model numbers and hostnames before they go public. Remove those strings and describe skills instead. Take down the old public text, not only the revision. Stop announcing maintenance windows in lobbies and tours. Crop photos that show whiteboards, badges, or screen names. Ask a reviewer who does not run the system what a stranger learns. Keep cafe-laptop and home-printing issues on the remote-work list. Do not call a monthly class this review. Recheck postings after a vendor or a product change. Write down the date the revealing text was removed.',
        'exam': 'Operational security means not leaking useful details such as system names, schedules, badge habits, or a door-controller model. A job post that drops the model and the remote-access hostname fits this label. A lobby mention of the Saturday maintenance window is the same leak. An unlocked laptop in a coffee shop is a hybrid-work habit, not this disclosure. Home printing is that neighboring habit too. A recurring class is a different program. The trap is treating every risky behavior as a public leak of internal detail.',
    },
    'Hybrid and remote-work habits': {
        'body': 'Hybrid and remote-work habits cover the office rules that have to travel with the person. Home networks, shoulder surfing in public, printing, and the places a company device may go are the substance. A laptop left unlocked in a coffee shop, with a chart on the screen, is a failure of these habits even when every job post is perfectly dull. Printing clinic records on a home printer puts paper where the clinic cannot retrieve it. A public conversation that reveals a hostname is a neighboring problem about what you say. The problem here is where the work happens and how the device and the paper are handled in that place.\n\nThe home network is not the clinic network. A clinic device should reach work through the approved path, and family machines should not become accidental file servers for charts. Shoulder surfing is ordinary in a cafe, on a train, and in a clinic waiting room after hours. The screen lock is the habit, and the angle of the screen matters when the work is visible to the next table. Printing rules should be blunt: records stay on clinic printers. A forgotten printout at home is not a convenience. It is a record in the wrong building. Travel rules should say whether the device may leave the country or sit in a checked bag. Vague advice to be careful will not answer a person packing on a Sunday night.\n\nA clinician left a laptop unlocked at a coffee shop on 18 September while waiting for an order. A chart was on the screen, and the person at the next table could read the name. The laptop was found by the clinician, not by a stranger who walked off with it, and the incident was still filed. The same month a supervisor stopped a plan to print discharge papers on a home printer the night before a visit. The papers were printed at the clinic in the morning instead. Neither event was a job post leaking a door-controller model. The security note kept the place, the device, and the data type, and it did not call the coffee shop a public-relations leak.\n\nA defender writes place-based rules people can follow on a Tuesday, checks that the approved remote path is the one actually used, and treats home printing as a data-location problem. Students fold this into operational security because both involve carelessness. The fact that separates these habits is place and handling: the device, the screen, the printer, and the network away from the controlled building.',
        'r0': 'Hybrid and remote habits govern home networks, public shoulder surfing, printing, and where a company device may go. On 18 September an unlocked clinic laptop showed a chart to the next table in a coffee shop, and the event was filed even though the laptop was not stolen. The same month a home-printing plan for discharge papers was stopped, and the papers were printed on a clinic printer in the morning. Work should use the approved remote path, not a family computer as a file server. Travel rules should say whether a device may leave the country or ride in a checked bag. A job post that reveals a hostname is a disclosure problem, not this place-based habit.',
        'r1': 'Office rules travel with the person. Lock the screen in a coffee shop or on a train. Do not leave a chart facing the next table. Do not print records on a home printer. Use the approved path back to work. Do not park charts on a family computer. Say where the device may travel. A checked bag is a decision, not a vibe. Vague advice to be careful is not a rule. A leaked hostname in a job post is different. That leak is about what you reveal. This habit is about place and handling. File the place, the device, and the data type.',
        'r2': "A baker who finishes orders at a cafe still keeps the recipe book shut when walking to the counter, and still refuses to run recipe pages through a home photocopier. The cafe is public even if the baker knows the staff. The home copier leaves pages in a house the shop cannot search. Using the shop's own delivery door, rather than a neighbor's open garage, is the approved way back. A flyer that names the shop's lock brand is a different mistake, made of words. In clinical work, the shut book is the locked screen and the home copier is the home printer. Hybrid and remote habits are these place and device rules, while a flyer that names the lock is operational security.",
        'r4': 'Lock screens before you step away in public. Keep sensitive screens angled away from the next table. Ban home printing of clinic records. Print those records on a clinic printer instead. Require the approved remote path for company devices. Forbid family computers as a store for charts. Write whether devices may travel or ride in checked bags. File incidents with the place, the device, and the data type. Do not label a coffee-shop screen as a job-post leak. Review the rules before a holiday travel week.',
        'exam': 'Hybrid and remote-work habits are the office rules that travel with the person: home networks, shoulder surfing, printing, and where devices may go. An unlocked laptop showing a chart in a coffee shop fits this label. Home printing of records fits this label. A job post that names a door controller or a hostname is operational security, a disclosure problem, not this one. Vague advice to be careful is not a rule. The approved remote path and a clinic printer are the practical counters. The trap is calling every leak a place problem.',
    },
    'Recurring awareness programs': {
        'body': "A recurring awareness program repeats training and measures whether behavior changes. One January video that nobody can describe by March is not a program. Repetition is the mechanism, and the measurement is what keeps the repetition honest. Short monthly topics beat a forgotten January recording. The count of people who report practice messages, or who repeat a specific mistake, is written down each month. If the count is weak, the next month's topic changes. A first-week practice for new hires can introduce the report button and the found-USB rule. That introduction is guidance. It becomes a program only when it returns on a calendar and someone looks at the result.\n\nThe owner, the topic list, and the measurement belong in one place. Topics should match incidents you are actually seeing, not a generic reel. If home printing showed up twice, the month can be about where paper may go. If job posts keep naming internal systems, the month can be about what a public sentence reveals. If shared passwords reappeared on a sticky note, the month can be about personal accounts. The practice message, when you use one, is there to teach and to count reports, not to shame a click in public. A discussion exercise can sit on the calendar too, and it should be labeled as a discussion so nobody confuses it with a live message that had to be reported.\n\nThrough the year the clinic replaced a single January video with twelve short monthly topics. Each topic fit in fifteen minutes. March covered the report button after a cluster of forwarded attachments. July covered home printing after two discharge packets were found on a home tray. The count of report-button uses on practice messages was recorded on the same sheet as attendance. When March's count was poor, April repeated the button instead of marching on to a new slogan. The January video file stayed in the archive and was not called the program. New-hire practice in week one continued, and the monthly sheet did not pretend that one welcome session covered the whole year.\n\nA defender owns the calendar, retires topics that no longer match incidents, and refuses to let a vendor's highlight reel become the curriculum by default. Students point at any training event and call it a program. The fact that separates a recurring program is the repeat plus the measurement, not a single class and not the detailed text of one habit.",
        'r0': 'A recurring program repeats lessons and measures behavior. Twelve short monthly topics, about fifteen minutes each, replaced one January video. March repeated the report-button lesson after forwarded attachments, and July covered home printing after two discharge packets were found on a home tray. The count of report-button uses on practice messages sat on the same sheet as attendance. A weak March count caused April to repeat the button rather than change the subject. New-hire practice in week one remained guidance, not the whole program. A discussion on the calendar must be labeled as a discussion so it is not mistaken for a live message. The owner changes the next topic when the measurement is weak.',
        'r1': 'Repeat the lesson on a calendar. Measure whether people do it. A once-a-year video is not a program. Keep topics short enough to finish. Match topics to incidents you actually see. Record attendance and the behavior count. If the count is weak, repeat the topic. Do not shame a click in public. A first-week welcome is only the start. Label a discussion as a discussion. Do not let a vendor reel choose the topics. Name an owner for the calendar. Change the month when the data is dull. Archive the old video without calling it the program.',
        'r2': "A kitchen that plays one safety film every January, which nobody can describe by February, does not have a safety practice. A kitchen that spends fifteen minutes each month on one real mistake, and writes down whether the mistake got rarer, does. If people are still leaving the walk-in door unlatched, the next month stays on that door instead of jumping to a new poster. A new cook's first morning still includes a tour, and the tour is not the monthly practice. In the clinic, the monthly fifteen minutes are the recurring awareness program and the written count is the measurement. The first-morning tour is guidance, and the forgotten January film is not a program just because it was scheduled once.",
        'r4': 'Replace a single annual video with short monthly topics. Name an owner for the calendar. Pick topics from incidents you are seeing now. Record attendance and a behavior count each month. Repeat a topic when the count is weak. Do not shame people who miss a practice message. Keep first-week practice labeled as guidance, not as the whole program. Label a discussion so it is not confused with a live report. Do not adopt a vendor reel by default. Archive the old video without calling it current. Review the topic list each quarter.',
        'exam': 'A recurring awareness program repeats training and measures behavior. Short monthly topics plus a written count fit this label. A single annual video that nobody remembers does not. First-week guidance can introduce a behavior and still is not the program, because it does not repeat and measure across the year. A weak count should change or repeat the next topic. Public shame is not a measurement. A vendor reel adopted by default is the trap. A discussion on the calendar must stay labeled as a discussion.',
    },
}
